00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:56.100
So, let me take you back
to the summer of 2014.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.520
When a lot of us were
horrified to see ISIS take over

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:03.010
large swathes of territory
in Northern Iraq and Syria.

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:05.220
That campaign involved
the ethnic cleansing

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.150
of religious minorities,
primarily Yazidis,

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:09.930
but also smaller numbers of Muslim

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:12.210
and Christian groups as well.

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:13.620
Hundreds of thousands of whom

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.600
were forcibly displaced
basically overnight

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:18.972
from this orange Iraqi area

00:03:18.972 --> 00:03:22.000
you can see on the left
there to the relative safety

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.283
of the green Kurdish held area.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.760
Many people thought that this
displacement would just last

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:33.360
for a few days and it was
actually two to three years

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.160
before they would return.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.520
And when they did return,
this is what they found.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.020
They found that their
churches, their schools,

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.810
their homes had been all but destroyed.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.650
These are two of the biggest
churches in the Christian city

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.863
of Qaraqosh in Northern Iraq.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:51.620
You can see the really heavy damage

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:52.803
from air raids.

00:03:53.803 --> 00:03:57.510
ISIS often would just torch
things and just burn them on

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:00.380
their way out when they
were forced out of the city.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.050
A lot of bullet holes everywhere,

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:04.300
and these pictures are
actually pretty recent.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.060
Like this place still looks like this,

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.363
even though the city was
liberated two years ago.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:13.300
And they also found similar
levels of damage to their homes.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:17.750
So, on the left is a picture
I took in a house in Qaraqosh.

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:20.590
You can see in the top left corner there,

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:22.600
the AC had been pried off the wall.

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:24.740
So, all the valuables were
gone when they came back,

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:27.200
like deeds to their houses, furniture,

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.700
really anything that could
be grabbed was grabbed.

00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:32.250
And on the floor,

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.430
you can see the beginnings of a tunnel

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.060
that had begun to be built by
ISIS fighters when they were

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:41.640
being closed in on before giving up

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:43.670
and on the right is another home

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:46.880
that I visited, also
a Christian residence.

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:48.550
And in graffiti, outside their house,

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:51.380
it says the Islamic State will live on.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:55.420
And maybe naively, I asked the
owners of this house, like,

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:58.690
why would you keep this ISIS
graffiti outside your house?

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:01.110
Like why don't you paint over that?

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:02.870
And they were very
adamant that they were not

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:03.980
gonna paint over it.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:08.330
And they wanted to preserve
this graffiti as a Testament

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:10.520
to the world of what had happened to them.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.260
And so this experience
is very much ingrained

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:16.100
in the Christian psyche and
in this communal identity,

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:18.310
this kind of collective
displacement experience

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:19.660
and this collective trauma.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:23.850
And because I think
that the people who live

00:05:23.850 --> 00:05:25.730
in these areas can speak for themselves,

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:27.320
maybe better than I can describe it.

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:31.810
I also have a short clip to show you,

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:33.490
and this is it Nomi,

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:35.470
who was actually one of
the study participants

00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:36.620
and his mom talking

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:38.520
about their displacement experience,

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:41.103
speaking to a PBS crew
who covered the study.

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:49.380
- Yana is one of thousands
that fled Kadakoosh

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:51.363
as ISIS approached.

00:05:51.363 --> 00:05:55.446
(mean speaking foreign language)

00:05:59.704 --> 00:06:03.787
(mean speaking foreign language)

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:05.800
Rami's mother thought

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:08.571
they would only be gone for a few days.

00:06:08.571 --> 00:06:12.738
(woman speaking foreign language)

00:06:14.637 --> 00:06:18.804
(woman speaking foreign language)

00:06:20.678 --> 00:06:24.845
(woman speaking foreign language)

00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:31.520
(woman speaking foreign language)

00:06:33.334 --> 00:06:35.460
- So, I think this shows just a tiny slice

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:37.300
of what these people went through.

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:40.720
And you can imagine that
after this kind of trauma

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.110
of that ISIS occupation

00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:44.780
just occupying your home
and just living there

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:46.280
and you come home and
that's what you find.

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:47.870
And so that displacement experience

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:51.390
and that very slow process of
return, as you can imagine,

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:54.670
has really devastated
social cohesion in the area,

00:06:54.670 --> 00:06:57.300
especially between Christians and Muslims.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:06:59.080
So, the Muslims who stayed behind

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.580
are seen as being ISIS collaborators,

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:02.950
and those who fled,

00:07:02.950 --> 00:07:05.700
even those who were themselves persecuted

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.210
because they belonged to
Muslim minority groups.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:09.960
They're resented for different reasons.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.570
They're seen as encroaching
on the Christian identity of

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:13.970
these areas and diluting

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:17.660
that historically Christian presence.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.480
So, whichever way you slice it,

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:20.960
whether they're seen
as ISIS collaborators,

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:22.760
or even if they are themselves victims,

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:23.760
that shared victim hood,

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:26.590
actually hasn't done much
to improve relations.

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:27.423
And so this leaves us

00:07:27.423 --> 00:07:29.300
with a really important policy problem,

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:31.001
which is how can we build social cohesion

00:07:31.001 --> 00:07:33.343
after this kind of violence?

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:38.370
And we have a set of tools
that potentially can help,

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:39.840
which is important.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:41.410
given that backsliding into violence

00:07:41.410 --> 00:07:44.140
after the physical fighting
has stopped, is common.

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:48.380
And so these tools can be
categorized as institutional tools

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:51.010
and tools at the grassroots
level very broadly.

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:52.200
So, at the institutional level,

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:55.250
you have things like power
sharing arrangements,

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:57.410
structural protections for minorities.

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:59.300
And then at the grassroots level,

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:03.180
we have programs that try to
improve everyday interactions

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:06.280
between individuals and from
those grassroots programs.

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:09.430
I'm focusing on one, which
is Intergroup contact.

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:11.830
This idea that positive
cooperative contact

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:16.110
across group lines generally
improves Intergroup relations.

00:08:16.110 --> 00:08:19.240
is thought to reduce prejudice
and build friendships.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:20.073
And on the whole,

00:08:20.073 --> 00:08:23.150
this is something that's
gonna take the society forward

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:25.260
in terms of social cohesion.

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:26.640
So, in recent years,

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:29.150
we've had a solid causal
evidence-based growing

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:29.983
on this question,

00:08:29.983 --> 00:08:31.670
but there's still a
lot that we don't know.

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:33.240
So, it seems generally positive,

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:35.720
but a lot of open questions remain.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:37.260
From those open questions

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:39.030
we don't know which conditions matter.

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:41.420
So, what type of contact matters?

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:43.990
The original theory says that
contact should be cooperative

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:45.000
and not competitive,

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.160
that it should be endorsed
by communal authorities

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:49.060
and norms, and that it should involve

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.260
equal power status between participants,

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:53.530
at least within the context
of the intervention,

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:55.550
even if there aren't equal in society

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:57.388
and of these conditions,

00:08:57.388 --> 00:08:59.788
we really only have good
causal evidence on one.

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:02.350
We also don't really know

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:06.370
whether contact can affect
real-world behaviors.

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:07.930
So, there's two problems here.

00:09:07.930 --> 00:09:11.640
The first is that the vast
majority of contact studies rely

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:13.930
on self-reported attitudes
measured directly

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:14.920
after the intervention.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:17.890
And so we don't really know
how robust these effects are.

00:09:17.890 --> 00:09:20.730
And the second is that
there is an assumption

00:09:20.730 --> 00:09:22.440
that's really core to contact theory,

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:25.230
which is that if you improve attitudes

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:27.640
and tolerance toward the contact partner,

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:30.033
so the people who come
together for the intervention

00:09:30.033 --> 00:09:34.100
that these positive acts will extend

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.230
to members of the out group more broadly.

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:38.560
So, you're now gonna navigate
the whole world differently.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:40.030
You're gonna treat strangers

00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:41.160
from the out group differently,

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.130
not just the people that you know,

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:46.400
and the thinking is
that contact is trivial

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:48.310
if the effects are concentrated only

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:50.470
within the people in the intervention,

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:52.140
but this assumption
that seen as really key

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:54.910
to understand the
effectiveness of this tool

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:57.330
and we don't really have systematic tests

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:59.560
between effects toward people

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:02.253
who meet in the intervention
versus more broadly.

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:06.060
And lastly, we don't
have a lot of evidence

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:08.730
for good reasons on contact after,

00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:12.020
during or after conflict for good reasons,

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:15.010
because obviously these
settings are hard to do work in,

00:10:15.010 --> 00:10:18.030
but also because the
likelihood of negative contact

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:20.248
or contact experience becoming negative

00:10:20.248 --> 00:10:22.730
is probably higher in these settings.

00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:24.770
And we do know that negative contact

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:26.000
disproportionately shapes

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.720
prejudice as opposed to positive contact.

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:31.250
And so the expectations
can go either way here.

00:10:31.250 --> 00:10:33.620
You could think baseline
tolerance is pretty low,

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:35.620
so there's a lot of room for improvement.

00:10:35.620 --> 00:10:36.810
On the other hand,

00:10:36.810 --> 00:10:38.230
you might also think that prejudice

00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:41.310
is particularly sticky in these settings.

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:43.010
Given that we do know that prejudice

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:44.900
is sticky among adults among those

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:46.870
who have been exposed to violence

00:10:46.870 --> 00:10:48.821
and when the type of
prejudice deals with ethnic

00:10:48.821 --> 00:10:50.750
or religious identities,

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:53.150
as opposed to prejudice toward the elderly

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:55.150
or the disabled, for example,

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:58.030
which is easier to shift
and all of these conditions

00:10:58.030 --> 00:10:59.833
are true in my particular setting.

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:05.480
So, what I'm gonna do, or what
I did do, is hold constant,

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:08.950
these optimal conditions of
contact as much as I can.

00:11:08.950 --> 00:11:09.910
So, this is pretty rough,

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:13.240
but generally this should be
a positive contact experience

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:16.620
via soccer, and team
sports naturally meet a lot

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:18.330
of the criteria I just mentioned.

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:19.630
So, it's cooperative.

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:23.140
You have this equalizing
effect of playing on a team

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:24.513
in terms of power status.

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:29.693
And the leagues were supported
by Christian leaders,

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:33.010
and I'm gonna hold
constant those conditions

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:35.810
and then test contact in
a post-conflict setting

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:39.810
and see whether a contact can
change real world behaviors.

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:42.966
So, can contact build tolerance after war

00:11:42.966 --> 00:11:46.850
and if so, do those effects extend outside

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:47.850
of the intervention?

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:51.720
And so the research question
is can contact build tolerance,

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:53.960
attitudes, and behaviors after war?

00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:55.420
And to preview the main result.

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:57.650
I found that contact improved

00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:00.070
everyday tolerant behaviors toward peers,

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:02.300
so toward the guys
within the intervention,

00:12:02.300 --> 00:12:05.200
but it did not necessarily
build broader social cohesion

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:06.033
outside of it.

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:09.560
So for he rest of the talk,

00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:11.330
I'm gonna outline the research design,

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:14.000
and then I'm gonna dive
straight into the main results.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:17.550
I'll discuss a couple of
interesting mechanisms

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:20.270
and then touch on
potential backlash effects,

00:12:20.270 --> 00:12:21.750
which are really important in this setting

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:23.770
and show how I ruled those out.

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:25.470
And then I want to open it up actually

00:12:25.470 --> 00:12:27.700
to get us talking in the discussion

00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:29.760
about trying to understand this pattern

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:31.880
of results and the implications,

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:35.120
and then I'll end by
discussing how much I think

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:39.260
the setting generalizes to
other settings and takeaways

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:41.273
from an academic and policy perspective.

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:44.740
So, jumping into the research design.

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:46.100
So, the aim of this study was

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:48.543
to build Christian
tolerance toward Muslims.

00:12:48.543 --> 00:12:51.030
If you are familiar with the Middle East,

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:52.880
this might seem a little bit backwards.

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:55.250
Like the broader social challenge

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:59.530
is improving Muslim
regard toward minorities

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:02.760
who do tend to be
stigmatized and marginalized.

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:04.330
The reason why I focus on Christians

00:13:04.330 --> 00:13:06.530
for this study is twofold.

00:13:06.530 --> 00:13:09.270
The first is that it's just practical.

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:12.320
I had access to this community
through my NGO partners

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:15.490
and they formed the majority
in these study sites.

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:17.340
And then the second reason is that there

00:13:17.340 --> 00:13:18.820
are anticipated benefits

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:21.880
to building social
cohesion within displaced,

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:23.750
among displaced people.

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:25.720
So, this seemed like something
that was worth doing.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:27.950
And especially given that
Christians are the majority

00:13:27.950 --> 00:13:30.290
in these areas, you still have in a way,

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:31.700
this more traditional dynamic

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:33.853
of attitudes toward minorities.

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:36.700
So, the sample consisted

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:40.520
of 42 entirely Christian soccer teams

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:42.390
across two study sites.

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:44.625
And so how this worked
was that I partnered

00:13:44.625 --> 00:13:48.980
with this Christian Iraqi
Christian community organization,

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:52.560
and we invited all of
the teams in the area,

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:55.290
which are almost entirely
divided by religion.

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:57.010
And we invited all the
Christian teams in the area

00:13:57.010 --> 00:13:58.070
to sign up for these leagues.

00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:00.470
And we really hyped it up.

00:14:00.470 --> 00:14:02.490
Like these leagues are gonna be awesome

00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:05.260
and the fields are new and
there's gonna be bleachers

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:07.250
and people in the stands and trophies

00:14:07.250 --> 00:14:10.333
and professional referees and
new uniforms and all of that.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:13.600
And so actually all of the teams

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:16.423
who were invited actually
accepted the invitation.

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:19.830
And so once they were recruited,

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:24.070
they then did a baseline survey
and based on their responses

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:28.000
to a baseline survey item
on empathy toward Muslims,

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:31.240
I did a block randomization
at the team level.

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:34.650
So, I took the team's average
response to this baseline item

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:37.090
on empathy, and then ranked the teams.

00:14:37.090 --> 00:14:41.100
And then all the two closest
teams kind of just paired

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:42.740
them one after the other,

00:14:42.740 --> 00:14:45.440
and then did this coin
flip to block randomize

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:46.940
at the team level in that way.

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:49.930
So, this is what the treatment
actually looked like.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:52.610
So, what happens if you
randomized the treatment?

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:53.870
So, on the left, you can see

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:58.870
that the control group received
additional Christian players

00:14:58.992 --> 00:15:00.950
and the treatment group received

00:15:00.950 --> 00:15:03.050
four additional Muslim players.

00:15:03.050 --> 00:15:05.260
And this holds constant the experience

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:07.040
of having new players added to your team.

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:08.860
So, these are preexisting teams

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:10.380
and they either get new Christian players

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:12.310
or new Muslim players.

00:15:12.310 --> 00:15:13.910
And these Christian Muslim players

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:17.270
who are added are themselves
drawn from the rosters

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:19.340
of teams who weren't
included at that first step.

00:15:19.340 --> 00:15:22.230
So, they didn't respond to the invitation,

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:24.230
or they were like just a little bit

00:15:24.230 --> 00:15:27.360
outside the geographic area,
or they were free agents,

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:29.350
or they weren't associated with a team.

00:15:29.350 --> 00:15:31.070
And so we're holding
constant this experience

00:15:31.070 --> 00:15:32.550
of training with a new player

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:35.020
who should be somewhat on
your level in terms of skill,

00:15:35.020 --> 00:15:37.630
because they're already
playing and practicing

00:15:37.630 --> 00:15:38.593
with other teams.

00:15:39.860 --> 00:15:43.090
And so the randomization
happens at two levels.

00:15:43.090 --> 00:15:45.250
The first is your treatment status,

00:15:45.250 --> 00:15:46.630
which is at the team level,

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:49.740
and then a conditional being
assigned to receive Christian

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:52.170
or Muslim players, those
individuals on your team,

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:54.083
they were also randomly assigned.

00:15:56.020 --> 00:16:01.020
So, this left me with four
leagues of roughly 14 teams each

00:16:01.530 --> 00:16:05.450
across these two study sites
and the teams trained together

00:16:05.450 --> 00:16:08.523
and they competed for a two month league.

00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:12.410
If you add up these training
hours and the playing hours,

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:14.840
they met maybe like two
or three times a week

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:16.460
for about two months.

00:16:16.460 --> 00:16:18.070
And so it was a league format.

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:21.080
So, every team played each
other. It was like a round Robin.

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:23.300
And then the teams with the
most points then advanced

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:25.830
to this knockout stage
of playing in the finals.

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:28.520
- [Man] Sorry, Salma, I just
needed some clarification.

00:16:28.520 --> 00:16:29.640
So, you just said, okay,

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:31.660
so the randomization at
the team level is whether

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:33.960
or not you get Christian
players or Muslim players added.

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:36.306
And then you said the conditional on that,

00:16:36.306 --> 00:16:39.980
the specific individuals
are randomized as well?

00:16:39.980 --> 00:16:41.110
I don't understand, okay,

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:43.160
how that affects the research design

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:46.400
or what kind of inferences
we can make from that.

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:47.480
- It doesn't affect the-.

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:50.420
It's more just to have some,

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:51.860
to feel a little bit more comfortable

00:16:51.860 --> 00:16:53.940
that these guys who are
being added to the team.

00:16:53.940 --> 00:16:55.680
So, it's kind of part of the treatment

00:16:55.680 --> 00:16:57.310
like who you're actually meeting.

00:16:57.310 --> 00:16:59.540
That there's no reason to think that like

00:17:00.475 --> 00:17:03.390
having Muslim players added
to your team would mean

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:05.720
they were systematically
better for example,

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:07.680
than the Christian players.

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:11.110
So, we get that inference from
randomized, from recruiting

00:17:11.110 --> 00:17:13.350
those added guys in the same way.

00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:15.120
So, they were from these rosters of teams

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:16.910
who weren't included at the first level,

00:17:16.910 --> 00:17:19.290
and then just randomizing
the particular individuals.

00:17:19.290 --> 00:17:21.163
Like it's not really that super important

00:17:21.163 --> 00:17:22.540
to the research study.

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:23.430
It's just to,

00:17:23.430 --> 00:17:25.370
hopefully the distribution of skills would

00:17:25.370 --> 00:17:27.370
be like somewhat even across the teams.

00:17:27.370 --> 00:17:29.925
And that is actually what we found

00:17:29.925 --> 00:17:31.350
when I look at the baseline ratings

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:33.203
of the skill as rated by the RAs.

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:40.730
Yeah, so, who are these guys?

00:17:40.730 --> 00:17:44.480
So, the median player is 23, unemployed,

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:48.220
not married and has a maximum
of a high school degree

00:17:48.220 --> 00:17:49.960
in terms of inter-group attitudes,

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:52.910
they have few to no Muslim friends.

00:17:52.910 --> 00:17:55.960
They would not be willing
to sell land to Muslims

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:59.160
and they would not want to
have a Muslim as a neighbor.

00:17:59.160 --> 00:18:01.350
How do they compare to
the general population

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:02.580
of displaced Christians?

00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:04.610
So, here I'm comparing the study sample,

00:18:04.610 --> 00:18:07.460
their baseline responses with a survey

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:10.410
of just over a thousand
displaced Christians,

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:13.130
which I supervised in the
area around the same time

00:18:13.130 --> 00:18:14.730
as the first study wave.

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:17.330
And so it seems generally
like along these items,

00:18:17.330 --> 00:18:19.760
like on the left here, you can see,

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:21.160
are you proud to be Iraqi?

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:23.510
Do you think coexistence is possible?

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:24.960
Do you think Muslims are welcoming?

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:26.620
Would you sell land to Muslims?

00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:27.500
They're a little bit,

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:29.760
they're pretty similar to
the general population.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:32.230
If anything, they're a
little bit less tolerant,

00:18:32.230 --> 00:18:34.550
a bit less likely to want to sell land

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:36.350
and view Muslims as being welcoming.

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:41.330
So, touching back on the research design.

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:43.278
So, we have this group
of guys they're about

00:18:43.278 --> 00:18:44.410
to start these leagues.

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:46.470
So, how do I actually
know if this is gonna work

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:48.150
and how do I know more importantly,

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:51.170
if the effects of contact
will extend off the field?

00:18:51.170 --> 00:18:54.260
So, I categorize my outcomes first,

00:18:54.260 --> 00:18:55.890
there's behavioral and
there's attitudinal.

00:18:55.890 --> 00:18:57.630
And within the behavioral outcomes,

00:18:57.630 --> 00:19:00.000
I look at on the field and
off the field behaviors,

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.621
which is supposed to
roughly map onto tolerance

00:19:02.621 --> 00:19:04.340
toward the guys who you know,

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:06.160
and then tolerance toward Muslim strangers

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:07.410
outside the intervention.

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:10.790
So, starting with the
on the field behaviors,

00:19:10.790 --> 00:19:13.340
the three behaviors I measure first,

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:16.910
how likely are you to register
for a mixed team next season?

00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:20.520
The second is whether you
vote for a Muslim player who

00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:24.080
is not on your team to
receive a sportsmanship prize.

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:25.750
And this award is really stressed

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:28.463
as being about sportsmanship,
not about skill,

00:19:29.450 --> 00:19:32.630
and if anything is actually
mechanistically biased in favor

00:19:32.630 --> 00:19:36.143
of choosing a Christian player,

00:19:36.143 --> 00:19:38.260
because you can't pick
someone on your team.

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:39.790
And so the guys on the treated teams,

00:19:39.790 --> 00:19:42.540
there were like four Muslims
who they couldn't select from.

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:45.210
So, it was a shorter
list of Muslims to pick.

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:47.260
And lastly, I look at
whether you are training

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:51.130
with Muslims six months later
after the intervention ended,

00:19:51.130 --> 00:19:53.530
and those Muslims could be
the guys who were assigned

00:19:53.530 --> 00:19:54.363
to your team,

00:19:54.363 --> 00:19:56.210
or it could be from the
neighborhood in general,

00:19:56.210 --> 00:19:57.770
the majority who were still training

00:19:57.770 --> 00:19:59.020
with Muslims six months later,

00:19:59.020 --> 00:20:02.560
were training with those who
were assigned to their teams.

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:03.890
So, these are my set of outcomes

00:20:03.890 --> 00:20:05.726
to reflect kind of this internal tolerance

00:20:05.726 --> 00:20:08.630
and moving to off the field tolerance,

00:20:08.630 --> 00:20:10.620
I capture three behaviors

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:13.520
that should measure behaviors
toward Muslim strangers

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:14.570
and how comfortable you are

00:20:14.570 --> 00:20:17.270
toward Muslims you don't know
outside the intervention.

00:20:17.270 --> 00:20:20.870
So, the first is whether you
attend a mixed social event.

00:20:20.870 --> 00:20:22.650
So, this was like a neighborhood event

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:24.900
that was put on by the league organizers.

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:28.770
And we made it very clear that
this was an open invitation

00:20:28.770 --> 00:20:30.667
to everyone in the neighborhood

00:20:30.667 --> 00:20:33.310
and that the Muslim
players would be coming,

00:20:33.310 --> 00:20:35.150
but also other Muslims from the community

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:36.680
and that they could
also bring their family

00:20:36.680 --> 00:20:38.020
and they could bring guests.

00:20:38.020 --> 00:20:39.120
And so it was very clear

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:43.220
that this reflected the
prospect of meeting Muslims

00:20:43.220 --> 00:20:45.570
who you didn't know necessarily,

00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:47.500
and actually in the first study wave,

00:20:47.500 --> 00:20:50.900
this event coincided with Ramadan
fast breaking meal, Iftar.

00:20:50.900 --> 00:20:54.340
So, it was even more salient for people

00:20:54.340 --> 00:20:56.930
that Muslims would be
coming to this event to eat.

00:20:56.930 --> 00:20:59.310
And just so if you're curious
what this event actually

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:02.078
looked like, here's like a little snippet.

00:21:02.078 --> 00:21:07.078
(video droning)
(upbeat music)

00:21:12.136 --> 00:21:17.136
(people speaking foreign language)

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:21.527
So, it was a dinner and
dancing and traditional dancing

00:21:21.527 --> 00:21:26.527
and some other games and
prizes were given out

00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:29.380
but it was not advertised
as a league event.

00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:31.660
It was advertised as this community event.

00:21:31.660 --> 00:21:34.430
The second behavior capturing
off the field behaviors

00:21:34.430 --> 00:21:37.330
is whether you patronize
a Muslim owned business

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:39.090
in a Muslim part of town.

00:21:39.090 --> 00:21:42.580
So, can a contact
overcome these structural

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:45.360
obstacles to social cohesion,

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:47.530
things like social segregation?

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:50.320
And so what I did was I had these coupons

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:51.840
and you can see a picture here.

00:21:51.840 --> 00:21:53.120
And so we gave all the players

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:55.200
with their unique ID, these coupons,

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:58.750
and these coupons were valid
for a restaurant in Mosul,

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:00.950
which is a Muslim dominated city

00:22:00.950 --> 00:22:04.790
that's about a 40 minute
drive from the study site.

00:22:04.790 --> 00:22:09.410
And it was valid for three
months after the league ended.

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:11.880
And so we then went and
collected these coupons again,

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:14.050
and then saw who actually
showed up cause it was stamped

00:22:14.050 --> 00:22:15.710
with their unique ID.

00:22:15.710 --> 00:22:18.300
So, are you more likely
to go to this Muslim town,

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:19.160
which was actually seen

00:22:19.160 --> 00:22:21.240
as the unofficial ISIS capital in Iraq?

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:22.610
So, it was very much associated,

00:22:22.610 --> 00:22:24.313
psychologically speaking with,

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:27.830
I guess like Muslim extremism

00:22:27.830 --> 00:22:30.541
or generally like not very
friendly toward Christians

00:22:30.541 --> 00:22:32.810
in the minds of the participants.

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:33.643
And then lastly,

00:22:33.643 --> 00:22:36.378
do you donate your $1 survey compensation

00:22:36.378 --> 00:22:39.130
to the church or do you donate it

00:22:39.130 --> 00:22:42.720
to a neutral NGO that serves
both Muslims and Christians?

00:22:42.720 --> 00:22:45.140
So, some of the examples
given more like orphanages

00:22:45.140 --> 00:22:48.543
and cancer charities versus the church.

00:22:52.010 --> 00:22:55.150
And then for attitudes, I have
eight items in the survey,

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:58.720
which I measure at baseline
and at the end line,

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:01.150
which took place about a
month after the intervention.

00:23:01.150 --> 00:23:05.350
And I use this hierarchical
clustering model to collapse

00:23:05.350 --> 00:23:08.020
these eight items into three indices.

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:10.880
And so I've labeled these
indices, national unity.

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:14.000
So, this consisted of items
like we should treat each other

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:16.450
as Iraqis first and ethnic

00:23:16.450 --> 00:23:20.060
and religious boundaries are arbitrary.

00:23:20.060 --> 00:23:22.220
The second index I label Muslim blame.

00:23:22.220 --> 00:23:24.080
So, how likely are you to blame

00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:26.279
these various Muslim civilian groups

00:23:26.279 --> 00:23:28.550
for Christian suffering?

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:29.610
And then lastly,

00:23:29.610 --> 00:23:31.710
would you want to have
any of these Muslim groups

00:23:31.710 --> 00:23:32.560
as your neighbor?

00:23:33.740 --> 00:23:36.260
So, I'll pause in case
anyone has further questions

00:23:36.260 --> 00:23:38.360
before I dive right into the main results.

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:42.640
Okay so, diving straight
into the main results.

00:23:42.640 --> 00:23:47.640
So, here I'm showing you
predicted probabilities adjusted,

00:23:48.250 --> 00:23:52.810
covariate adjusted for the
six outcomes I just described.

00:23:52.810 --> 00:23:55.540
So, first comparing the means
for treatment and control.

00:23:55.540 --> 00:23:58.860
So, if we look at the, on
the field outcomes first,

00:23:58.860 --> 00:24:00.970
so training with Muslims,

00:24:00.970 --> 00:24:04.130
voting for a Muslim to
receive a sportsmanship award

00:24:04.130 --> 00:24:06.570
and registering from
mixed team next season,

00:24:06.570 --> 00:24:07.830
we consistently see

00:24:07.830 --> 00:24:11.560
these significant positive
treatment effects.

00:24:11.560 --> 00:24:14.330
And then when we move to
the off the field outcomes.

00:24:14.330 --> 00:24:16.290
So, do you visit this Muslim restaurant

00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:17.330
in another part of town?

00:24:17.330 --> 00:24:19.260
Do you go to this mixed social event?

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:20.180
Do you donate

00:24:20.180 --> 00:24:23.763
your compensation to a mixed
NGO as opposed to the church?

00:24:24.730 --> 00:24:27.060
There's not much movement there at all.

00:24:27.060 --> 00:24:29.480
So, this suggests that contact

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:31.830
seems to be effective for on the field

00:24:31.830 --> 00:24:32.780
or internal tolerance,

00:24:32.780 --> 00:24:35.560
but it at least seems to struggle
in this case to generalize

00:24:35.560 --> 00:24:37.503
to other social settings

00:24:37.503 --> 00:24:40.543
and looking at the attitudinal results.

00:24:40.543 --> 00:24:43.430
They're not displayed here,
but of the three indices.

00:24:43.430 --> 00:24:44.763
I didn't see much movement
on Muslim neighbor

00:24:44.763 --> 00:24:49.270
or Muslim blame, but I
did see this positive,

00:24:49.270 --> 00:24:52.810
pretty chunky effect of
the national unity index.

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:55.950
So, it was very abstract
ideas about ethnic divisions

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:57.980
being arbitrary and the
need to treat each other

00:24:57.980 --> 00:24:59.280
as Iraqis first.

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:00.920
But when you ask about really concrete,

00:25:00.920 --> 00:25:02.490
inter-group attitudes questions,

00:25:02.490 --> 00:25:03.920
like how do you feel about Muslims?

00:25:03.920 --> 00:25:06.030
We don't really see a change, which again,

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:09.530
seems to suggest that I'm
looking at this generalized

00:25:09.530 --> 00:25:11.530
treatment of Muslims didn't really move,

00:25:11.530 --> 00:25:13.140
but the guys who you
met in the intervention,

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:15.320
that that was more malleable.

00:25:15.320 --> 00:25:18.460
So, are these two classes
of outcomes on the field

00:25:18.460 --> 00:25:20.270
and off the field we see here that

00:25:20.270 --> 00:25:21.250
there seems to be a pattern,

00:25:21.250 --> 00:25:24.260
but can we actually test
these against each other?

00:25:24.260 --> 00:25:26.040
So, here's an exploratory analysis

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:28.393
that I did trying to answer that question.

00:25:29.300 --> 00:25:33.060
So, what I did here was I estimated

00:25:33.060 --> 00:25:35.920
six new average treatment effects.

00:25:35.920 --> 00:25:38.570
So, first I look at the
treatment effect of engaging

00:25:38.570 --> 00:25:41.530
in at least in one on the field behavior

00:25:41.530 --> 00:25:42.560
and then the treatment effect

00:25:42.560 --> 00:25:44.800
for engaging on one
off the field behavior.

00:25:44.800 --> 00:25:46.430
And then I test those
two against each other.

00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:48.680
And then I do the same
for whether you engage

00:25:48.680 --> 00:25:51.200
in two on the field behaviors
or two off the field,

00:25:51.200 --> 00:25:54.513
find those two ATEs, test
those against each other,

00:25:56.090 --> 00:25:58.780
and then do the same
for all three behaviors.

00:25:58.780 --> 00:26:01.080
So, once I do that and I'm able

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:04.170
to actually directly
compare the on the field

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:05.300
and off the field behaviors

00:26:05.300 --> 00:26:06.810
and do this difference in difference.

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:09.860
So, the two difference
in difference groups here

00:26:09.860 --> 00:26:11.630
being first treatment and control,

00:26:11.630 --> 00:26:13.040
and the second level of differences

00:26:13.040 --> 00:26:15.560
on the field versus off the field,

00:26:15.560 --> 00:26:17.610
there does seem to be this pattern

00:26:17.610 --> 00:26:20.060
that actually seems to be
statistically significant,

00:26:20.060 --> 00:26:22.900
that your contact does actually make you

00:26:22.900 --> 00:26:25.742
more likely to engage in on the field

00:26:25.742 --> 00:26:27.540
as opposed to off the field behavior.

00:26:27.540 --> 00:26:29.880
So, this pattern seems to be robust,

00:26:29.880 --> 00:26:31.450
in just that first viewing.

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:33.270
- [Man] Can I ask a question?

00:26:33.270 --> 00:26:34.103
- Yes.

00:26:34.103 --> 00:26:35.070
- [Man] Can you hear me?

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:36.440
- Yes.

00:26:36.440 --> 00:26:38.290
- [Man] Actually I have two questions

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:42.370
on this aspect of the analysis.

00:26:42.370 --> 00:26:45.480
I guess I don't fully understand the way

00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:48.720
you do the hour on the
field, off the field.

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:52.690
The more natural procedure
would have been, you know,

00:26:52.690 --> 00:26:56.080
maybe there is some sort of latent factor

00:26:56.080 --> 00:26:58.530
which represents on the field behavior.

00:26:58.530 --> 00:27:02.080
You have six markers of it and
you do a factor analysis on

00:27:02.080 --> 00:27:04.270
those and you do something similar

00:27:04.270 --> 00:27:08.450
for the other type of behavior.

00:27:08.450 --> 00:27:11.040
And so I don't fully
understand this one or two,

00:27:11.040 --> 00:27:14.870
like why is that the most
efficient way of doing it?

00:27:14.870 --> 00:27:17.210
The other one is more substantive.

00:27:17.210 --> 00:27:21.020
So, this is a technical
suggestion at this point.

00:27:21.020 --> 00:27:22.480
Maybe you can improve that.

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:25.630
The other one, I don't
see what you're mentioning

00:27:25.630 --> 00:27:30.083
with this difference,
this probably my common

00:27:31.090 --> 00:27:33.200
lack of understanding of these things,

00:27:33.200 --> 00:27:36.700
but so why is it interesting
to look at that the differences

00:27:36.700 --> 00:27:39.230
in these two factors in these two,

00:27:39.230 --> 00:27:42.397
like in variables on the
field, off the field?

00:27:44.560 --> 00:27:45.393
- Yeah, sure.

00:27:45.393 --> 00:27:48.400
So, I think this factor analysis,

00:27:48.400 --> 00:27:52.030
I think that's also
another technique I'm open

00:27:52.030 --> 00:27:55.200
to exploring different ways
of getting at this question.

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:57.410
This is kind of a way that I came up with,

00:27:57.410 --> 00:27:59.960
I'm sure there are
others what I think works

00:27:59.960 --> 00:28:00.910
about this approach.

00:28:00.910 --> 00:28:03.840
So, just to maybe elaborate
on the motivation.

00:28:03.840 --> 00:28:05.490
So, just going back to this,

00:28:05.490 --> 00:28:07.710
the plot for the main results,

00:28:07.710 --> 00:28:09.910
usually you see a plot where like,

00:28:09.910 --> 00:28:12.170
let's say you have a pattern
of null results somewhere

00:28:12.170 --> 00:28:13.385
and then results somewhere else.

00:28:13.385 --> 00:28:15.780
And we usually just leave it at that.

00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:17.217
Like we see a pattern
here and a pattern here.

00:28:17.217 --> 00:28:19.270
And so I wanted to take it a step further

00:28:19.270 --> 00:28:21.790
and test those patterns
against each other,

00:28:21.790 --> 00:28:23.200
to understand if they're significant

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:24.900
when you test them against each other.

00:28:24.900 --> 00:28:26.037
And going back to the theory, like,

00:28:26.037 --> 00:28:28.460
you know, why am I doing this study?

00:28:28.460 --> 00:28:29.790
One of the core motivations

00:28:29.790 --> 00:28:32.057
is to know whether contact works just

00:28:32.057 --> 00:28:33.840
for the people who you know,

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:35.196
like these known contacts,

00:28:35.196 --> 00:28:37.460
or does it actually have the potential

00:28:37.460 --> 00:28:40.750
to unlock this broader
cohesion towards strangers?

00:28:40.750 --> 00:28:42.640
And so it's good to know
that there's a pattern

00:28:42.640 --> 00:28:44.900
of null results here and it
seems more effective here,

00:28:44.900 --> 00:28:45.733
but to be able

00:28:45.733 --> 00:28:47.170
to test those two things
against each other,

00:28:47.170 --> 00:28:51.260
I think provides more
information for getting

00:28:51.260 --> 00:28:54.478
at that core question of
whether effects generalize.

00:28:54.478 --> 00:28:56.730
Yeah, I think there's plenty
of ways of doing this.

00:28:56.730 --> 00:28:59.330
I like the idea of doing well,

00:28:59.330 --> 00:29:00.890
obviously I like it because I did it.

00:29:00.890 --> 00:29:03.304
I liked the idea of classifying
the outcomes in terms

00:29:03.304 --> 00:29:05.420
of engaging in one or more behaviors,

00:29:05.420 --> 00:29:09.490
two or more, just descriptively
like this panel on the left.

00:29:09.490 --> 00:29:12.090
I think it's interesting to
see like the distribution

00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:15.220
of how many of these
outcomes people engaged in.

00:29:15.220 --> 00:29:17.820
I think another way of
doing it would be pairwise,

00:29:17.820 --> 00:29:19.800
like compare all of the

00:29:20.720 --> 00:29:24.420
on the field outcomes and all
of the off the field outcomes

00:29:24.420 --> 00:29:25.760
and compare them all against each other

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:27.660
without classifying them in this way.

00:29:27.660 --> 00:29:29.252
I just thought this was
kind of a neat structure

00:29:29.252 --> 00:29:32.683
to understand a little bit
descriptively what was going on.

00:29:33.560 --> 00:29:35.500
So, like you can see
for all three behaviors,

00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:37.410
like very few people engaged in those.

00:29:37.410 --> 00:29:40.590
Like it's kind of a sanity
check that these magnitudes

00:29:40.590 --> 00:29:42.230
are going in same direction as

00:29:42.230 --> 00:29:44.921
we're kind of asking more of
the participants at each step.

00:29:44.921 --> 00:29:47.200
Yeah, but I think there's
other ways of doing it.

00:29:47.200 --> 00:29:49.583
I think factor analysis would work too.

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:54.630
- [Man] Thanks.

00:29:54.630 --> 00:29:55.530
- Yeah. Thank you.

00:29:56.753 --> 00:30:01.280
Okay, we seem to see here
that contact is effective,

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:03.970
at least for some behavioral outcomes,

00:30:03.970 --> 00:30:06.620
more mixed effects for
attitudes and no effects

00:30:06.620 --> 00:30:10.120
for off the field outcomes or how people

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:12.290
are behaving toward Muslim strangers.

00:30:12.290 --> 00:30:14.180
So, why could that be the case?

00:30:14.180 --> 00:30:18.790
So, here are some secondary
analysis that I ran,

00:30:18.790 --> 00:30:21.170
mapping out what does
the literature tell us

00:30:21.170 --> 00:30:24.570
in terms of generating
expectations about mechanisms?

00:30:24.570 --> 00:30:27.500
And so two of the mechanisms
that loom really large

00:30:27.500 --> 00:30:28.530
in the contact literature,

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:31.860
the first is that contact
might provide more information.

00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:34.147
And I don't find evidence for that.

00:30:34.147 --> 00:30:39.147
So, the treatment had no effect
on information about Muslims

00:30:40.864 --> 00:30:44.503
and when you also break
apart the treatment effects

00:30:44.503 --> 00:30:46.510
by subgroup and look at those

00:30:46.510 --> 00:30:48.900
who had like low versus
high baseline information.

00:30:48.900 --> 00:30:50.560
I also don't see anything happening there.

00:30:50.560 --> 00:30:55.200
So, it doesn't seem to be the
key factor in this setting.

00:30:55.200 --> 00:30:57.110
The second mechanism in the literature

00:30:57.110 --> 00:31:00.340
is that contact might induce empathy.

00:31:00.340 --> 00:31:03.460
And similarly, I don't find
evidence for this here either.

00:31:03.460 --> 00:31:05.670
So, again, looking at
empathy as an outcome,

00:31:05.670 --> 00:31:09.010
or whether you look at
heterogeneous treatment effects

00:31:09.010 --> 00:31:12.603
by baseline empathy, I also
don't see a difference there.

00:31:14.370 --> 00:31:17.340
I do find some evidence
for two other mechanisms.

00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:20.330
So, a mixture of qualitative
and quantitative evidence.

00:31:20.330 --> 00:31:23.260
The first is that having a
positive experience seems

00:31:23.260 --> 00:31:24.433
to be important.

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:27.582
So, being on a team that ends up

00:31:27.582 --> 00:31:29.960
being successful seems to be important

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:33.240
for amplifying treatment effects.

00:31:33.240 --> 00:31:35.830
And lastly, it seems to be that contact

00:31:35.830 --> 00:31:39.580
was changing norms around
the accessibility of contact,

00:31:39.580 --> 00:31:42.093
both among players and among residents.

00:31:43.060 --> 00:31:44.680
So, all right.

00:31:44.680 --> 00:31:46.210
- [Alex] Can I just ask you, does,

00:31:46.210 --> 00:31:49.090
does the treatment effect
the success of the teams?

00:31:49.090 --> 00:31:51.070
- I was just waiting
for this I'm like, yes.

00:31:51.070 --> 00:31:54.344
Alex is gonna ask me if it
team's success is post treatment.

00:31:54.344 --> 00:31:55.645
(Alex laughs)

00:31:55.645 --> 00:31:57.743
- Yes please! Is it post treatment?

00:31:57.743 --> 00:32:02.743
- Yeah, so, on the one
hand, the treatment status

00:32:03.543 --> 00:32:06.310
doesn't actually correlate
with your success.

00:32:06.310 --> 00:32:07.726
So, it doesn't seem like

00:32:07.726 --> 00:32:10.965
being on mixed teams made you play better.

00:32:10.965 --> 00:32:12.819
When I also rerun this analysis,

00:32:12.819 --> 00:32:16.740
looking at when I
instrument for team success

00:32:16.740 --> 00:32:19.957
with baseline skill rating of the players,

00:32:19.957 --> 00:32:21.931
I also see similar results.

00:32:21.931 --> 00:32:24.920
But yeah, I think it's
kind of in a gray area.

00:32:24.920 --> 00:32:28.730
I think it is post-treatment
in that the actual playing

00:32:28.730 --> 00:32:30.130
together happened post-treatment

00:32:30.130 --> 00:32:32.750
but I think it's kind
of part of the bundle,

00:32:32.750 --> 00:32:33.750
so if you wanna-.

00:32:33.750 --> 00:32:35.701
- [Alex] Yeah, it is
not true that the teams

00:32:35.701 --> 00:32:39.694
that got four extra Christian
players scored more goals

00:32:39.694 --> 00:32:41.557
or won more games?

00:32:41.557 --> 00:32:42.561
- Right.

00:32:42.561 --> 00:32:45.040
- [Alex] Or had fewer
penalties, or whatever.

00:32:45.040 --> 00:32:47.350
- Yeah, so actually I
don't have it in here,

00:32:47.350 --> 00:32:50.810
but in the paper I look
at success in three ways.

00:32:50.810 --> 00:32:53.260
So, first, like the number
of points your team scored,

00:32:53.260 --> 00:32:57.020
secondly, the skill rating of the players

00:32:57.020 --> 00:32:58.180
and third was the number

00:32:58.180 --> 00:33:00.530
of goals scored and actually
on all three of those,

00:33:00.530 --> 00:33:02.590
just doing a T test between
treatment and control,

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:04.490
there doesn't seem to be a difference.

00:33:05.500 --> 00:33:07.860
Yeah, so, and actually I'll
get into this in a second.

00:33:07.860 --> 00:33:10.760
So, I'll just dive in
now to this team success

00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:13.030
and also these changing
norms around contact.

00:33:13.030 --> 00:33:15.200
So, starting first with
this changing norms.

00:33:15.200 --> 00:33:17.350
So, it seemed from this
observational evidence

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:22.070
that at least in the very,
very limited, I guess,

00:33:24.540 --> 00:33:28.360
the level of just the norms
around training with Muslims,

00:33:28.360 --> 00:33:30.230
like actually playing soccer with people

00:33:30.230 --> 00:33:32.760
who are not Christian,
like this very basic thing

00:33:32.760 --> 00:33:34.130
that didn't really used to happen before

00:33:34.130 --> 00:33:35.860
these leagues seems to have shifted.

00:33:35.860 --> 00:33:38.490
So, here's a quote from one of
the Christian players saying,

00:33:38.490 --> 00:33:39.950
you know, honestly, before this league,

00:33:39.950 --> 00:33:42.100
you would never see Muslims
playing with a Christian team.

00:33:42.100 --> 00:33:43.150
Like this is the first time.

00:33:43.150 --> 00:33:46.440
And this is reflected in
the baseline data as well.

00:33:46.440 --> 00:33:49.350
So, of the total of 51 teams in the study,

00:33:49.350 --> 00:33:50.930
only two of them at baseline said

00:33:50.930 --> 00:33:53.113
that they regularly train with Muslims.

00:33:54.680 --> 00:33:57.760
Another, I said, when the game
was over, we hug and kiss,

00:33:57.760 --> 00:33:59.790
even when we lose, that
was really a huge thing.

00:33:59.790 --> 00:34:02.434
So, these micro gestures
of just being friendly

00:34:02.434 --> 00:34:04.166
were things that were actually

00:34:04.166 --> 00:34:08.410
not normalized before we took these steps

00:34:08.410 --> 00:34:10.010
to actually integrate the teams.

00:34:11.020 --> 00:34:12.320
And again, because I think the players

00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:13.680
can describe it better than I can,

00:34:13.680 --> 00:34:16.350
a short clip of some of
the players themselves.

00:34:16.350 --> 00:34:19.140
The first guy is actually
a Christian team captain.

00:34:19.140 --> 00:34:21.763
And then the other is a Muslim goalkeeper.

00:34:22.788 --> 00:34:26.788
(man speaking foreign language)

00:34:30.093 --> 00:34:34.093
(man speaking foreign language)

00:34:35.628 --> 00:34:39.628
(man speaking foreign language)

00:34:42.750 --> 00:34:44.490
So, it seemed at least among the guys

00:34:44.490 --> 00:34:45.960
who knew each other and they met

00:34:45.960 --> 00:34:48.214
and some friendships
grew out of this in ways

00:34:48.214 --> 00:34:51.733
that would be predicted
by the contact hypothesis.

00:34:54.040 --> 00:34:56.693
And I also have some suggestive evidence

00:34:56.693 --> 00:35:00.030
of spillover effects
among local residents.

00:35:00.030 --> 00:35:01.790
So, here on the left,
you can see a picture

00:35:01.790 --> 00:35:02.830
of a league game.

00:35:02.830 --> 00:35:05.400
And so these really drew a lot of crowds.

00:35:05.400 --> 00:35:06.790
This was also in the first wave

00:35:06.790 --> 00:35:09.330
before we got the funding
to upgrade the fields.

00:35:09.330 --> 00:35:11.580
So, just don't judge me on the AstroTurf,

00:35:11.580 --> 00:35:13.540
but a lot of people came out to these,

00:35:13.540 --> 00:35:15.130
like there were hundreds of people.

00:35:15.130 --> 00:35:20.130
And so I ran this survey of
about 150 local residents

00:35:20.240 --> 00:35:21.850
like randomly selected.

00:35:21.850 --> 00:35:24.480
And we asked them about their
exposure to the leagues,

00:35:24.480 --> 00:35:25.870
which I measured in a few ways.

00:35:25.870 --> 00:35:28.388
So, do you live within
walking distance of a field?

00:35:28.388 --> 00:35:31.540
Did you go to the final game,

00:35:31.540 --> 00:35:33.840
which had a lot of fanfare and TV crews

00:35:33.840 --> 00:35:35.403
and the prize ceremony,

00:35:36.310 --> 00:35:38.530
do you have a family or a friend playing

00:35:38.530 --> 00:35:42.790
on the teams and how
many games did you attend

00:35:42.790 --> 00:35:43.990
if you attended games?

00:35:43.990 --> 00:35:46.560
So, these are obviously
completely correlational,

00:35:46.560 --> 00:35:48.922
so maybe more tolerant people
actually show up to the games,

00:35:48.922 --> 00:35:52.510
but there does seem to be some
kind of positive correlation

00:35:52.510 --> 00:35:54.230
between your exposure to the leagues

00:35:54.230 --> 00:35:57.270
and your inter-group attitudes.

00:35:57.270 --> 00:35:58.680
So, this first plot here,

00:35:58.680 --> 00:36:01.792
so looking first on the X axis,

00:36:01.792 --> 00:36:04.557
if you attended the final game,

00:36:04.557 --> 00:36:06.520
it seems like the people who attended

00:36:06.520 --> 00:36:10.360
had a more positive view
of the league's impact

00:36:10.360 --> 00:36:11.830
on their community than those

00:36:11.830 --> 00:36:12.940
who didn't come to the final.

00:36:12.940 --> 00:36:15.660
And we invited everyone
in the town to show up,

00:36:15.660 --> 00:36:18.340
and then this was asked
actually three months

00:36:18.340 --> 00:36:19.490
after the final.

00:36:19.490 --> 00:36:21.906
And then the plot on the
right just looks at going

00:36:21.906 --> 00:36:26.300
from I think the second or no,

00:36:26.300 --> 00:36:31.300
the first to the fourth quartile
in terms of games attended

00:36:31.769 --> 00:36:34.560
and looking at what your likelihood

00:36:34.560 --> 00:36:38.170
of saying that you support
future inter-group programming

00:36:38.170 --> 00:36:39.003
in the town.

00:36:39.003 --> 00:36:40.080
So, the examples that were given

00:36:40.080 --> 00:36:43.662
were women's volleyball
tournament's and gardening clubs.

00:36:43.662 --> 00:36:45.863
So, again, totally correlational.

00:36:47.070 --> 00:36:49.100
But I think it's important
that this highlights

00:36:49.100 --> 00:36:51.830
that spill over effects and
these peripheral effects

00:36:51.830 --> 00:36:54.940
that happen outside of the
specific contact intervention

00:36:54.940 --> 00:36:55.810
are really important

00:36:55.810 --> 00:36:58.540
if we care about amplifying
the impact beyond

00:36:58.540 --> 00:37:00.740
just the specific people
in the intervention,

00:37:00.740 --> 00:37:04.140
especially since it seems
like even among those people,

00:37:04.140 --> 00:37:06.060
it doesn't necessarily seem to be the case

00:37:06.060 --> 00:37:07.630
that contact effects generalized.

00:37:07.630 --> 00:37:09.900
So, we're trying to think like engineers,

00:37:09.900 --> 00:37:12.450
how to amplify the impact
of this intervention

00:37:12.450 --> 00:37:14.850
that only touches a very
small number of people.

00:37:16.670 --> 00:37:18.311
So, moving on to team success.

00:37:18.311 --> 00:37:20.500
So, I touched on this earlier,

00:37:20.500 --> 00:37:22.570
but it really seemed to be
important that if you played

00:37:22.570 --> 00:37:24.410
on a successful team and if the Muslims

00:37:24.410 --> 00:37:25.991
on your team played well,

00:37:25.991 --> 00:37:27.812
that this had an impact

00:37:27.812 --> 00:37:30.380
on how people viewed the entire endeavor.

00:37:30.380 --> 00:37:33.650
And so I got thinking about
this when on the sidelines,

00:37:33.650 --> 00:37:35.840
like the Christian guy was on the bench

00:37:35.840 --> 00:37:37.650
and he saw the Muslim player

00:37:37.650 --> 00:37:39.490
on one of the teams
score like a hat trick.

00:37:39.490 --> 00:37:42.770
And he just turns to the research
team or the league staff.

00:37:42.770 --> 00:37:43.603
And he's like,

00:37:43.603 --> 00:37:45.060
why didn't you give us a
Muslim that plays like that?

00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:46.510
Like instead of, you know,

00:37:46.510 --> 00:37:48.390
points like the Christian guy on his team

00:37:48.390 --> 00:37:49.460
who doesn't play like that.

00:37:49.460 --> 00:37:52.560
And so effects really,

00:37:52.560 --> 00:37:54.340
I wouldn't say they're
contingent on the Muslims

00:37:54.340 --> 00:37:55.630
on your team not sucking,

00:37:55.630 --> 00:37:59.330
but it definitely helps to
have a similar skill level

00:37:59.330 --> 00:38:01.560
so that you don't build resentment.

00:38:01.560 --> 00:38:02.393
And so here,

00:38:02.393 --> 00:38:05.320
I'm just breaking out the effects

00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:06.850
of playing on a successful team,

00:38:06.850 --> 00:38:08.170
just among the treated group.

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:10.640
And this is just a really
simple difference in means.

00:38:10.640 --> 00:38:13.970
So, again, looking on the
X, zero and one just means,

00:38:13.970 --> 00:38:16.010
are you on a successful team or not?

00:38:16.010 --> 00:38:17.689
And I'm measuring success
by reaching the finals.

00:38:17.689 --> 00:38:19.820
So, did you reach the knockout stage?

00:38:19.820 --> 00:38:22.170
You were like the best in your league.

00:38:22.170 --> 00:38:25.010
And then on the Y I'm
showing just the proportion

00:38:25.010 --> 00:38:28.846
of people on the treated
teams who did that outcome.

00:38:28.846 --> 00:38:31.970
So, there seems to be
this consistent pattern,

00:38:31.970 --> 00:38:35.520
even on outcomes that
reflect generalized tolerance

00:38:35.520 --> 00:38:37.399
of playing on a successful team.

00:38:37.399 --> 00:38:40.118
And this pretty much across the board,

00:38:40.118 --> 00:38:42.830
when you formalize this a little bit more

00:38:42.830 --> 00:38:44.480
like in the covariate adjusted model,

00:38:44.480 --> 00:38:45.440
you see the same thing.

00:38:45.440 --> 00:38:47.720
So, it seems like the
successful teams really did

00:38:47.720 --> 00:38:49.093
have amplified effects.

00:38:51.120 --> 00:38:53.950
- [Alex] Sorry, you
mentioned that you also,

00:38:53.950 --> 00:38:56.714
it's Lushvig's first question.

00:38:56.714 --> 00:38:58.819
What was going on with that
individual random assignment?

00:38:58.819 --> 00:38:59.840
So, this is what's going on

00:38:59.840 --> 00:39:01.300
with the individual
random assignment, right?

00:39:01.300 --> 00:39:04.420
Is that you're basically
getting a random draw,

00:39:04.420 --> 00:39:06.650
whether you got the good
ones or the bad ones.

00:39:06.650 --> 00:39:08.653
And I guess I'm interested in,

00:39:09.920 --> 00:39:12.100
in order to understand this conditioning.

00:39:12.100 --> 00:39:13.090
So, like how much of,

00:39:13.090 --> 00:39:15.990
how much did the skill
of the players vary?

00:39:15.990 --> 00:39:19.490
Like how much worse was the worst set

00:39:19.490 --> 00:39:21.603
of four than the best set of four?

00:39:23.190 --> 00:39:25.960
- Yeah, so, how I'm trying to get a skill

00:39:25.960 --> 00:39:28.270
is like looking at the
number of goals scored

00:39:28.270 --> 00:39:31.770
by added players and by the skill rating,

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:33.400
like how RAs rate their skill

00:39:33.400 --> 00:39:34.900
and maybe they seem to be balanced,

00:39:34.900 --> 00:39:36.760
but you still do have individuals

00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:39.660
that can end up being stars in other ways.

00:39:39.660 --> 00:39:41.860
Like those two measures
are obviously imperfect.

00:39:41.860 --> 00:39:44.040
So, you might have like
a really good defender,

00:39:44.040 --> 00:39:44.980
or a really good goalkeeper,

00:39:44.980 --> 00:39:46.620
and then that wouldn't be measured

00:39:46.620 --> 00:39:49.113
in one of those metrics, for example.

00:39:51.200 --> 00:39:52.821
So, I think you do have individual stars

00:39:52.821 --> 00:39:55.490
and maybe there's a way to look at people

00:39:55.490 --> 00:39:56.430
on those extremes

00:39:56.430 --> 00:39:58.210
rather than just look at the average level

00:39:58.210 --> 00:39:59.650
of the players who were added,

00:39:59.650 --> 00:40:02.600
that actually I think
is a really good idea.

00:40:02.600 --> 00:40:04.720
Yeah, maybe something like goals contested

00:40:04.720 --> 00:40:07.580
as a measure for like defenders

00:40:07.580 --> 00:40:10.420
and to look at like the star, star players

00:40:10.420 --> 00:40:13.560
and look at where they, so the
top, like three goals scores,

00:40:13.560 --> 00:40:14.630
what team they were on or something

00:40:14.630 --> 00:40:17.083
to get at more of the extreme variation.

00:40:18.020 --> 00:40:19.100
- [Man] So, this relates

00:40:19.100 --> 00:40:21.170
to an important external validity point.

00:40:21.170 --> 00:40:24.130
Let's hope my internet
connection holds up.

00:40:24.130 --> 00:40:26.140
So, in South Africa, you know,

00:40:26.140 --> 00:40:29.780
traditionally cricket and
rugby have been white sports,

00:40:29.780 --> 00:40:32.012
especially cricket I know well

00:40:32.012 --> 00:40:36.560
and whereas soccer has been
a primarily black sport

00:40:36.560 --> 00:40:39.060
and the cricket board has tried hard

00:40:39.060 --> 00:40:42.520
to integrate more and have
more black players come in.

00:40:42.520 --> 00:40:45.250
And so they've actually had the equivalent

00:40:45.250 --> 00:40:46.540
of affirmative action in order

00:40:46.540 --> 00:40:49.490
to get more black players
in through the ranks.

00:40:49.490 --> 00:40:51.650
And that's actually
created a lot of resentment

00:40:51.650 --> 00:40:55.670
because they were on
average, not as good, right?

00:40:55.670 --> 00:40:57.850
And there was a lot of
resentment about white players

00:40:57.850 --> 00:40:59.930
who were like, no, we try hard,

00:40:59.930 --> 00:41:02.290
and we try to, you know,

00:41:02.290 --> 00:41:04.070
we practice a lot and we try to get good

00:41:04.070 --> 00:41:06.520
and then we don't make the teams

00:41:06.520 --> 00:41:08.130
and we don't earn as much money.

00:41:08.130 --> 00:41:09.907
- That's exactly right.

00:41:09.907 --> 00:41:13.050
I think one of the reasons
why this league had so much

00:41:13.050 --> 00:41:16.170
buy in and such little dropout is

00:41:16.170 --> 00:41:18.490
because we made it really competitive

00:41:18.490 --> 00:41:20.425
and really prestigious
and really attractive,

00:41:20.425 --> 00:41:22.666
and people were really
invested in doing well.

00:41:22.666 --> 00:41:25.860
And so regardless of the
religious background,

00:41:25.860 --> 00:41:26.806
like they want to win.

00:41:26.806 --> 00:41:29.226
And so the flip side of that
is that I think you have

00:41:29.226 --> 00:41:33.020
to make sure where there's
some minimum level of skill

00:41:33.020 --> 00:41:34.630
or else you will get,

00:41:34.630 --> 00:41:36.960
I think you will expect backlash effects

00:41:36.960 --> 00:41:39.630
and you will expect
resentment because you're

00:41:39.630 --> 00:41:42.610
like a net negative in
these people's lives.

00:41:42.610 --> 00:41:43.520
And so I think it's already, I mean,

00:41:43.520 --> 00:41:47.580
one of the drawbacks as well
of using pre-existing teams

00:41:47.580 --> 00:41:50.280
is that they have this, like,
they're much more tight knit.

00:41:50.280 --> 00:41:51.640
And so when you add a new player,

00:41:51.640 --> 00:41:55.100
you already have that risk of resentment

00:41:55.100 --> 00:41:56.360
at having someone come in,

00:41:56.360 --> 00:41:58.390
who they haven't chosen when everyone else

00:41:58.390 --> 00:41:59.540
on their team has,

00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:01.890
they're like a tightly run machine

00:42:01.890 --> 00:42:03.650
they've been training each other,

00:42:03.650 --> 00:42:04.600
they know each other well,

00:42:04.600 --> 00:42:06.770
and now you're throwing
in this random person.

00:42:06.770 --> 00:42:08.870
So, that's one of the drawbacks.

00:42:08.870 --> 00:42:10.340
Another way to do this study would be

00:42:10.340 --> 00:42:12.000
to randomly construct the teams.

00:42:12.000 --> 00:42:13.910
Like you actually put the
teams together from scratch,

00:42:13.910 --> 00:42:16.600
where this might not be as
much of an issue potentially,

00:42:16.600 --> 00:42:18.600
but then you get less buy in I think.

00:42:18.600 --> 00:42:19.433
- [Alex] Does this kinda respond

00:42:19.433 --> 00:42:21.220
to any of Allport's conditions?

00:42:21.220 --> 00:42:24.270
This like need that your
person that you're cooperating

00:42:24.270 --> 00:42:25.833
with has to be any good at it?

00:42:27.867 --> 00:42:29.790
It seems like a new
condition that you're adding

00:42:29.790 --> 00:42:31.390
to the Contact Hypothesis,
which is like, good.

00:42:31.390 --> 00:42:32.930
We should move past all of it,

00:42:32.930 --> 00:42:36.420
like we haven't, I don't know.

00:42:36.420 --> 00:42:39.270
We haven't like tested all
four of the conditions.

00:42:39.270 --> 00:42:41.523
So, is this a fifth one?

00:42:42.848 --> 00:42:44.850
- I mean, I think his original
conditions are reasonable,

00:42:44.850 --> 00:42:47.567
but I think it's not
like, you know, the Bible

00:42:47.567 --> 00:42:50.400
or the Quaran, you know,
we can move past it.

00:42:50.400 --> 00:42:54.500
So, I think for any endeavor
where like the outcome matters

00:42:54.500 --> 00:42:55.660
and there's a stake in it,

00:42:55.660 --> 00:42:57.660
and it's something that's, skill-based,

00:42:57.660 --> 00:43:00.920
I think you have to have
some minimum amount of skill.

00:43:00.920 --> 00:43:04.320
Does that map onto like, having
the experience be positive?

00:43:04.320 --> 00:43:07.360
I think that's part of it, but
it could be something deeper.

00:43:07.360 --> 00:43:10.110
Like it could be related
to like the actual outcome,

00:43:10.110 --> 00:43:12.430
like success in a more objective
sense, rather than just,

00:43:12.430 --> 00:43:13.680
did you have fun doing it?

00:43:13.680 --> 00:43:16.888
And success doesn't map
perfectly onto having fun.

00:43:16.888 --> 00:43:18.930
Like maybe it's even just to show

00:43:18.930 --> 00:43:22.100
that a successful experience is possible,

00:43:22.100 --> 00:43:24.130
like that counters people's stereotypes.

00:43:24.130 --> 00:43:26.620
Like I think it could reflect
a lot of things, but yeah.

00:43:26.620 --> 00:43:27.540
I mean, if it were up to me,

00:43:27.540 --> 00:43:30.640
I would add, if it's a skill-based
venture or if it's based

00:43:30.640 --> 00:43:33.350
on meritocracy, you should
ensure a minimum level

00:43:33.350 --> 00:43:36.252
of meritocracy or else you're
gonna build resentment.

00:43:36.252 --> 00:43:38.540
Lushvig, what do you think?

00:43:38.540 --> 00:43:40.290
- [Lushvig] So, yeah, I was wondering,

00:43:40.290 --> 00:43:43.810
I'm sure you know Matt Lowe's
work at UBC much better

00:43:43.810 --> 00:43:47.120
than I do, his experiment was

00:43:47.120 --> 00:43:49.790
about randomly constructing
teams, is that right?

00:43:49.790 --> 00:43:51.183
Rather than this?
- Yes.

00:43:52.304 --> 00:43:54.530
Yeah. So, I think he, yeah,

00:43:54.530 --> 00:43:57.510
it's a fantastic study and
I like love when we present

00:43:57.510 --> 00:43:58.906
our work side by side.

00:43:58.906 --> 00:44:01.364
Yeah. He actually put the teams together.

00:44:01.364 --> 00:44:03.800
And I think in that setting,

00:44:03.800 --> 00:44:05.960
you had a lot of buy-in for other reasons.

00:44:05.960 --> 00:44:07.670
Like, I don't need to tell you like

00:44:07.670 --> 00:44:08.880
how much people like cricket

00:44:08.880 --> 00:44:10.870
and he set it up to be
like very professional.

00:44:10.870 --> 00:44:13.550
And so I think in some
settings it's possible,

00:44:13.550 --> 00:44:15.450
and you don't have so much this drawback

00:44:15.450 --> 00:44:19.487
of less of an investment, but
in this setting in particular,

00:44:19.487 --> 00:44:21.745
I think people just would have, like,

00:44:21.745 --> 00:44:26.260
there was already this amateur leagues

00:44:26.260 --> 00:44:28.090
and team structure that
was already happening.

00:44:28.090 --> 00:44:31.057
And it felt a little bit
unnatural to move outside of that

00:44:31.057 --> 00:44:33.100
and ask people to forget about their teams

00:44:33.100 --> 00:44:34.850
and start from scratch.

00:44:34.850 --> 00:44:36.950
And it was already a very
big ask in this setting

00:44:36.950 --> 00:44:39.320
to get them to accept having
Muslim players on their team.

00:44:39.320 --> 00:44:40.660
There's a lot of pushback around that.

00:44:40.660 --> 00:44:42.850
So, one of the concessions was like, okay,

00:44:42.850 --> 00:44:45.110
we keep the teams as they are.

00:44:45.110 --> 00:44:48.000
- And then finally Gotham Row's work,

00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:50.730
which was on Delhi schools, you know,

00:44:50.730 --> 00:44:53.390
where that was natural
variation where low caste kids

00:44:53.390 --> 00:44:56.782
or civil class, like disadvantaged kids

00:44:56.782 --> 00:44:59.593
were put in these classrooms.

00:45:00.720 --> 00:45:03.730
I would love to, after the talk

00:45:03.730 --> 00:45:05.000
or like at some point

00:45:05.000 --> 00:45:06.400
when you have time to write an op-ed,

00:45:06.400 --> 00:45:11.400
it would be great to get a
sense of like a two pager,

00:45:11.835 --> 00:45:14.060
you know, what did we
learn from the combination

00:45:14.060 --> 00:45:17.590
of all these studies, given
the differences in them?

00:45:17.590 --> 00:45:19.560
Like with the combination, of course.

00:45:19.560 --> 00:45:21.010
- Yeah, that's a great idea.

00:45:21.010 --> 00:45:23.070
I know that J-Pal is asking like,

00:45:23.070 --> 00:45:26.650
me and Matt are doing some
kind of like evidence Roundup

00:45:26.650 --> 00:45:29.200
about contact, so I think
an op-ed is a great idea.

00:45:30.740 --> 00:45:32.340
Yeah, I love the route paper as well

00:45:32.340 --> 00:45:34.570
because he does actually
look at real world behaviors

00:45:34.570 --> 00:45:36.940
and he also looks at academic
achievement as an outcome,

00:45:36.940 --> 00:45:39.294
which I think speaks
to this other question

00:45:39.294 --> 00:45:42.840
of how does diversity or
introduce contact effect

00:45:42.840 --> 00:45:46.820
like success or these like
measurable attainment metrics.

00:45:46.820 --> 00:45:48.402
And I kind of touch on it here,

00:45:48.402 --> 00:45:50.810
but it's such an
important question outside

00:45:50.810 --> 00:45:53.476
of just the standard
prejudice related outcomes.

00:45:53.476 --> 00:45:56.150
Yeah. That's a great idea.

00:45:56.150 --> 00:45:58.793
I'm gonna commit myself to doing that.

00:46:01.779 --> 00:46:03.860
- [Woman] Stephen had
the question on the chat.

00:46:03.860 --> 00:46:04.723
- Yeah, please.

00:46:09.274 --> 00:46:10.610
- [Stephen] It wasn't a question.

00:46:10.610 --> 00:46:12.000
It was just an observation

00:46:12.000 --> 00:46:13.550
that if you're trying to make the parallel

00:46:13.550 --> 00:46:16.760
to real societies, then
having pre-existing teams

00:46:16.760 --> 00:46:18.500
is a good thing.

00:46:18.500 --> 00:46:21.248
- Yeah, it's much more naturalistic,

00:46:21.248 --> 00:46:23.683
naturalistic, natural.

00:46:24.560 --> 00:46:25.900
It's much less artificial

00:46:25.900 --> 00:46:27.490
that these guys are
already playing together.

00:46:27.490 --> 00:46:29.330
And then you have the
drawback of maybe a lack

00:46:29.330 --> 00:46:31.030
of generalizability or other things,

00:46:31.030 --> 00:46:34.197
or you can't really tinker in
the way that you might want.

00:46:34.197 --> 00:46:35.940
But yeah, I completely agree.

00:46:35.940 --> 00:46:38.253
Like it was as unintrusive as possible.

00:46:39.201 --> 00:46:41.420
- I mean, we mentioned

00:46:41.420 --> 00:46:44.333
just the original Contact
Hypothesis as well.

00:46:44.333 --> 00:46:46.720
So, as I remember most of it

00:46:46.720 --> 00:46:49.043
tasks and that were sort of group things

00:46:49.043 --> 00:46:51.820
like you all push the
truck together rather

00:46:51.820 --> 00:46:54.110
than an individual skills-based thing.

00:46:54.110 --> 00:46:59.110
So, that does seem to
be some new dimension.

00:46:59.835 --> 00:47:02.403
- Yeah, I mean, I'll
get to this a little bit

00:47:02.403 --> 00:47:05.820
at the end in terms of generalizability

00:47:05.820 --> 00:47:06.860
and how we should update.

00:47:06.860 --> 00:47:10.052
But one of the key Allport's conditions,

00:47:10.052 --> 00:47:11.580
from the original hypothesis

00:47:11.580 --> 00:47:14.230
is that the contact should be cooperative.

00:47:14.230 --> 00:47:15.500
So, it should involve some degree

00:47:15.500 --> 00:47:17.650
of cooperation as opposed to competition.

00:47:17.650 --> 00:47:18.970
So, if you play against each other,

00:47:18.970 --> 00:47:21.340
you're not gonna find these effects,

00:47:21.340 --> 00:47:24.340
which is what Matt Lowe
tests very well in his paper.

00:47:24.340 --> 00:47:25.580
And then the other condition,

00:47:25.580 --> 00:47:28.418
which I never quite figured
out the actual difference,

00:47:28.418 --> 00:47:29.655
but there's another condition,

00:47:29.655 --> 00:47:32.630
which is that you should be
working toward a common goal.

00:47:32.630 --> 00:47:34.750
So, cooperation and having a common goal

00:47:34.750 --> 00:47:37.743
are supposed to be like two
of the critical conditions.

00:47:41.270 --> 00:47:42.103
- [Lushvig] And, you know,

00:47:42.103 --> 00:47:45.690
thinking about policy
implications in other settings

00:47:45.690 --> 00:47:48.750
that are consequential, you know,

00:47:48.750 --> 00:47:53.120
maybe getting refugees and
the host communities, right.

00:47:53.120 --> 00:47:56.090
If they have to do a project
together, like, you know,

00:47:56.090 --> 00:47:57.670
getting them to work together towards

00:47:57.670 --> 00:47:59.250
like rebuilding efforts or paying both

00:47:59.250 --> 00:48:02.766
of them to work in the
same team, this might-.

00:48:02.766 --> 00:48:04.060
- Exactly right.

00:48:04.060 --> 00:48:06.410
I mean, there's no, there's nothing unique

00:48:06.410 --> 00:48:08.070
to contact that suggests that it has

00:48:08.070 --> 00:48:10.650
to only be this like
inter-religious prejudice

00:48:10.650 --> 00:48:12.008
that can be overcome.

00:48:12.008 --> 00:48:14.320
You can easily apply this to like migrants

00:48:14.320 --> 00:48:16.650
and refugees, different kinds of refugees,

00:48:16.650 --> 00:48:19.970
really any cleavage of
identity-based difference

00:48:19.970 --> 00:48:22.811
where that identity is known
by everyone in the room.

00:48:22.811 --> 00:48:25.360
There's no reason why it shouldn't work.

00:48:25.360 --> 00:48:28.330
And I think that's like the next frontier.

00:48:28.330 --> 00:48:30.980
And I know you guys have Sandra
Roseau's presenting soon.

00:48:30.980 --> 00:48:33.520
And me and her actually
have a project testing,

00:48:33.520 --> 00:48:37.820
a very similar setup in Columbia
with Venezuelan migrants.

00:48:37.820 --> 00:48:39.320
And like, is there something different

00:48:39.320 --> 00:48:41.490
about having newly mixed
populations as opposed

00:48:41.490 --> 00:48:44.300
to these old neighbors
that I have in my setting?

00:48:44.300 --> 00:48:47.620
That essentially would change
our expectations, but yeah,

00:48:47.620 --> 00:48:50.713
there's really no limits to
it if I may say so myself.

00:48:52.726 --> 00:48:55.149
Okay so, I know I want to
leave some time for discussion,

00:48:55.149 --> 00:48:57.700
so I'll just dive back in.

00:48:57.700 --> 00:48:59.990
So, I explained a little
bit about what happened

00:48:59.990 --> 00:49:01.610
with the guys on the treated teams.

00:49:01.610 --> 00:49:04.240
And so now I want to touch on
the guys in the control teams.

00:49:04.240 --> 00:49:06.010
So, these were guys on
all Christian teams,

00:49:06.010 --> 00:49:09.720
can we rule out backlash or
at least large enough backlash

00:49:09.720 --> 00:49:12.210
to be detected, given my sample size?

00:49:12.210 --> 00:49:13.790
And I want to show that that's the case.

00:49:13.790 --> 00:49:15.840
I think it's really important
in these fragile settings

00:49:15.840 --> 00:49:17.830
where you have this higher
risk of negative contact

00:49:17.830 --> 00:49:21.010
to be very aware of this possibility.

00:49:21.010 --> 00:49:24.701
So, the first way I rule out
backlash is looking at team,s

00:49:24.701 --> 00:49:27.900
looking at games, this
is match level data.

00:49:27.900 --> 00:49:30.720
So, matches that brought
together a mixed team

00:49:30.720 --> 00:49:32.310
with an all Christian team

00:49:32.310 --> 00:49:34.870
versus matches that brought
together two Christian teams

00:49:34.870 --> 00:49:36.200
or two mixed teams.

00:49:36.200 --> 00:49:39.040
And the idea is that we
want to rule out that an all

00:49:39.040 --> 00:49:41.010
Christian team facing off and competing

00:49:41.863 --> 00:49:42.696
against a mixed team,

00:49:42.696 --> 00:49:43.900
that they are more
aggressive on the field.

00:49:43.900 --> 00:49:47.400
So, it seems to be the case.

00:49:47.400 --> 00:49:48.660
So, it doesn't seem to be a difference

00:49:48.660 --> 00:49:50.669
in the number of red
cards or yellow cards.

00:49:50.669 --> 00:49:53.913
But if you're looking at
Christian versus mixed team

00:49:53.913 --> 00:49:57.220
compared to two Christian
teams or two mixed teams.

00:49:57.220 --> 00:49:59.750
Using cards here is
some kind of rough proxy

00:49:59.750 --> 00:50:01.640
for aggression on the field.

00:50:01.640 --> 00:50:05.223
So, like fouls and bad language,
and that kind of thing.

00:50:06.796 --> 00:50:08.500
Another way I can rule out backlash

00:50:08.500 --> 00:50:10.110
is looking just at the control team.

00:50:10.110 --> 00:50:12.080
So, these are the guys
on all Christian teams

00:50:12.080 --> 00:50:15.310
and comparing their attitudes over time.

00:50:15.310 --> 00:50:17.260
So, their baseline and
their end line surveys,

00:50:17.260 --> 00:50:19.132
and there doesn't seem
to be much of a change.

00:50:19.132 --> 00:50:20.472
It looks like kind of a wash.

00:50:20.472 --> 00:50:21.976
And so this is good.

00:50:21.976 --> 00:50:24.050
So, this suggests that
at least they didn't

00:50:24.050 --> 00:50:27.053
become like significantly
more prejudice over time.

00:50:28.560 --> 00:50:31.520
I can do the same for the
Muslim participants as well.

00:50:31.520 --> 00:50:33.610
So, obviously it's a much smaller sample.

00:50:33.610 --> 00:50:35.995
So, some of this lack of
precision is just due to that,

00:50:35.995 --> 00:50:40.420
but it also seems like there
wasn't like a huge increase in

00:50:40.420 --> 00:50:42.500
their prejudice toward Christians

00:50:42.500 --> 00:50:45.560
when we look at their end line
compared to their baseline.

00:50:45.560 --> 00:50:46.650
- [Lushvig] Hey was there a coach

00:50:46.650 --> 00:50:48.920
who was making consequential decisions

00:50:48.920 --> 00:50:51.740
on the field, like
substitutes and support?

00:50:51.740 --> 00:50:52.740
- Yes.

00:50:52.740 --> 00:50:56.320
- [Lushvig] And was that
person typically Christian?

00:50:56.320 --> 00:50:58.880
- They were always
Christian, the coaches, yeah.

00:50:58.880 --> 00:51:01.520
- [Lushvig] Okay, so then
why not look at outcomes

00:51:01.520 --> 00:51:05.627
like the number of amount
of playing time the Muslims

00:51:05.627 --> 00:51:09.243
were given versus the
Christian and things like that?

00:51:09.243 --> 00:51:10.240
- Yeah, that's a good idea.

00:51:10.240 --> 00:51:13.230
We actually had in the
rules of the league,

00:51:13.230 --> 00:51:14.830
like when we'd brief the coaches,

00:51:14.830 --> 00:51:17.800
we said that they had to have
some kind of equal rotation

00:51:17.800 --> 00:51:20.570
and that players had to get
roughly equal playing time.

00:51:20.570 --> 00:51:22.138
So, we had this like rotating substitute.

00:51:22.138 --> 00:51:26.880
So, these guys were like,
not in the best shape.

00:51:26.880 --> 00:51:28.900
So, they had to rotate really regularly.

00:51:28.900 --> 00:51:31.010
And it was like a very
high intensity format.

00:51:31.010 --> 00:51:32.900
So, that actually turned out
to not be a problem at all.

00:51:32.900 --> 00:51:34.560
Like we had like unlimited substitutions,

00:51:34.560 --> 00:51:35.942
they were always rotating.

00:51:35.942 --> 00:51:39.630
But in general, I think
this is a great idea.

00:51:39.630 --> 00:51:42.810
Especially given the importance
of like communal leaders

00:51:42.810 --> 00:51:44.550
and norms and endorsing the contact.

00:51:44.550 --> 00:51:47.130
The coaches are kind of one
of the key figures in all of

00:51:47.130 --> 00:51:48.812
this to make it happen.

00:51:48.812 --> 00:51:50.070
- [Lushvig] And the fact that performance

00:51:50.070 --> 00:51:51.410
didn't actually, you know,

00:51:51.410 --> 00:51:52.900
there was no significant
difference in performance

00:51:52.900 --> 00:51:55.220
is actually I would say a positive.

00:51:55.220 --> 00:51:57.053
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:51:58.590 --> 00:52:00.350
Oh gosh. I lost Lushvig.

00:52:01.873 --> 00:52:03.278
- [Lushvig] Got lost again.

00:52:03.278 --> 00:52:04.111
- Oh, you're back.

00:52:04.111 --> 00:52:04.944
- [Lushvig] Okay. Sorry.

00:52:04.944 --> 00:52:08.250
I was saying that like, it
might've been a positive outcome.

00:52:08.250 --> 00:52:09.500
- Yeah.

00:52:09.500 --> 00:52:11.800
I guess it depends what
your expectations are.

00:52:12.730 --> 00:52:13.563
Like if the,

00:52:13.563 --> 00:52:15.820
if the counterfactuals that
it would get much worse

00:52:15.820 --> 00:52:18.190
and like things would just
spiral out of control then yeah,

00:52:18.190 --> 00:52:19.023
it's good, yeah,

00:52:19.023 --> 00:52:22.290
so maybe like a null in
this case is a good thing,

00:52:22.290 --> 00:52:23.846
but the coaches were actually key.

00:52:23.846 --> 00:52:27.210
One thing that we observed
was at the beginning,

00:52:27.210 --> 00:52:28.930
when we first integrated the teams,

00:52:28.930 --> 00:52:31.120
like the Christian players were speaking

00:52:31.120 --> 00:52:33.264
their native dialect, not Arabic.

00:52:33.264 --> 00:52:36.120
So, they purposely were like,
excluding the Muslim players.

00:52:36.120 --> 00:52:37.650
They wouldn't really sit
next to them on the bench.

00:52:37.650 --> 00:52:39.880
Like the players were
feeling kind of excluded.

00:52:39.880 --> 00:52:41.610
And I was telling the
research manager, like,

00:52:41.610 --> 00:52:42.450
are you sure this is okay?

00:52:42.450 --> 00:52:43.640
Like, I feel bad for these guys.

00:52:43.640 --> 00:52:44.473
He's like, it's okay.

00:52:44.473 --> 00:52:45.890
Just let them train, give
them a week to train.

00:52:45.890 --> 00:52:47.330
We'll do an orientation.

00:52:47.330 --> 00:52:50.360
And slowly you could see it.
They warmed up to each other.

00:52:50.360 --> 00:52:52.840
But the coaches actually had
a key intervention, which is,

00:52:52.840 --> 00:52:55.650
they told their players,
this is unprompted from us.

00:52:55.650 --> 00:52:57.770
They said, we have to all speak Arabic

00:52:57.770 --> 00:52:59.360
from now on we're speaking Arabic

00:52:59.360 --> 00:53:00.970
so that way everyone can
understand what's going on.

00:53:00.970 --> 00:53:02.960
And that was kind of a turning
point for everything else.

00:53:02.960 --> 00:53:06.811
So, I totally agree that like
engaging coaches and seeing,

00:53:06.811 --> 00:53:10.689
looking at their decision-making
is really consequential.

00:53:10.689 --> 00:53:11.914
So, to wrap up,

00:53:11.914 --> 00:53:15.340
so we have this pattern
of results that suggests

00:53:15.340 --> 00:53:17.720
that contact effects are really
concentrated among people

00:53:17.720 --> 00:53:18.553
who know each other.

00:53:18.553 --> 00:53:20.480
And so why could that be the case?

00:53:20.480 --> 00:53:22.010
There's a lot of different structures

00:53:22.010 --> 00:53:24.035
you could overlay on this result.

00:53:24.035 --> 00:53:27.053
So, here are some potential explanations.

00:53:27.053 --> 00:53:30.333
One is that it could just
be too much of an ask.

00:53:30.333 --> 00:53:33.380
So, it could be like
after the kind of trauma

00:53:33.380 --> 00:53:35.020
that these people have experienced,

00:53:35.020 --> 00:53:37.390
asking them to then start
trusting Muslim strangers

00:53:37.390 --> 00:53:38.910
is just a step too far.

00:53:38.910 --> 00:53:40.049
So, for example,

00:53:40.049 --> 00:53:43.000
if you look at this young man
in the bottom left corner,

00:53:43.000 --> 00:53:45.030
he brought this picture

00:53:45.030 --> 00:53:48.310
to one of the final games
of his family member,

00:53:48.310 --> 00:53:49.460
I believe was his father

00:53:49.460 --> 00:53:51.060
who had passed away in the fighting.

00:53:51.060 --> 00:53:52.400
And this was actually quite common.

00:53:52.400 --> 00:53:54.440
Like people who would
bring these framed photos

00:53:54.440 --> 00:53:57.130
and then on the right,
you see the same guy like

00:53:57.130 --> 00:53:59.070
with his Muslim teammate, like joking,

00:53:59.070 --> 00:54:00.704
laughing like a few minutes later.

00:54:00.704 --> 00:54:03.487
And in his mind, there's no,

00:54:03.487 --> 00:54:08.050
there's no disconnect
between those two things.

00:54:08.050 --> 00:54:09.850
Like you can still have the views

00:54:09.850 --> 00:54:12.530
that you have toward Muslims more broadly,

00:54:12.530 --> 00:54:14.505
and the kind of suffering

00:54:14.505 --> 00:54:16.989
that you underwent is ingrained in you,

00:54:16.989 --> 00:54:18.917
but you can meet people
and learn to trust them

00:54:18.917 --> 00:54:20.730
and like them and have
friends from that group.

00:54:20.730 --> 00:54:23.660
And you don't necessarily see that link.

00:54:23.660 --> 00:54:25.980
And so it might be that these kinds

00:54:25.980 --> 00:54:27.620
of grassroots programs need

00:54:27.620 --> 00:54:29.410
those structural policy levers,

00:54:29.410 --> 00:54:31.110
like things like structural protections

00:54:31.110 --> 00:54:34.010
and like real real policy change

00:54:34.010 --> 00:54:36.470
to be combined with this grassroots stuff,

00:54:36.470 --> 00:54:38.460
to really unlock the positive effects.

00:54:38.460 --> 00:54:39.710
Like if he really felt safe,

00:54:39.710 --> 00:54:41.390
going to Mosul and he felt safe

00:54:41.390 --> 00:54:42.700
going about his day-to-day life,

00:54:42.700 --> 00:54:44.883
maybe contact would have a bigger effect.

00:54:45.820 --> 00:54:47.190
Secondly, it might be the case that

00:54:47.190 --> 00:54:49.230
off the field behaviors
are actually difficult

00:54:49.230 --> 00:54:51.644
to compare to on the field behaviors

00:54:51.644 --> 00:54:54.180
and that they're more costly.

00:54:54.180 --> 00:54:56.150
So, one advantage of this study

00:54:56.150 --> 00:54:57.640
is that I'm testing these new set

00:54:57.640 --> 00:54:59.740
of real-world behaviors,
but at the same time,

00:54:59.740 --> 00:55:02.960
because they're very
natural and realistic,

00:55:02.960 --> 00:55:05.760
the drawback is that they're a
little bit less standardized,

00:55:05.760 --> 00:55:07.510
so it's hard to compare them directly.

00:55:07.510 --> 00:55:10.610
So, for example, going
to Mosul is a bigger ask

00:55:10.610 --> 00:55:12.540
than voting for someone
to receive an award

00:55:12.540 --> 00:55:15.739
like going to Mosul requires
driving time, getting gas,

00:55:15.739 --> 00:55:19.534
going through annoying checkpoints
and that kind of thing.

00:55:19.534 --> 00:55:22.280
It might also be that again,

00:55:22.280 --> 00:55:23.715
back to my example of this young man,

00:55:23.715 --> 00:55:27.080
that the guys who you meet
in the contact setting,

00:55:27.080 --> 00:55:29.357
you don't view them as
typical for whatever reason.

00:55:29.357 --> 00:55:32.780
So, it kind of is baked into
the theory that in order

00:55:32.780 --> 00:55:35.590
for contact to work and
generalize outside the setting,

00:55:35.590 --> 00:55:38.170
you should see the people
who you're dealing with

00:55:38.170 --> 00:55:40.490
as being typical or else
you're gonna do all sorts

00:55:40.490 --> 00:55:42.871
of mental, like self inpretzeling

00:55:42.871 --> 00:55:46.387
to say that they are exceptions.

00:55:46.387 --> 00:55:48.220
So, if you don't see them as typical,

00:55:48.220 --> 00:55:49.630
then why would you generalize?

00:55:49.630 --> 00:55:52.520
You don't see these two groups
of people as being the same,

00:55:52.520 --> 00:55:54.191
so you're not gonna make any inferences.

00:55:54.191 --> 00:55:56.966
And lastly, I find that

00:55:56.966 --> 00:56:00.210
within like looking at
kind of zooming out at

00:56:00.210 --> 00:56:02.103
all the outcomes I measure,
behaviors and attitudes,

00:56:02.103 --> 00:56:04.900
that the results are more
concentrated among behaviors

00:56:04.900 --> 00:56:06.660
in general, I saw much more weak

00:56:06.660 --> 00:56:08.430
or mixed effects on attitudes.

00:56:08.430 --> 00:56:10.520
This actually aligns with other work

00:56:10.520 --> 00:56:12.210
from post-conflict settings,

00:56:12.210 --> 00:56:14.130
like in Rwanda and between Christians

00:56:14.130 --> 00:56:18.160
and Muslims in Nigeria, also
looking at prejudice reduction.

00:56:18.160 --> 00:56:20.370
And there seems to be this emerging,

00:56:20.370 --> 00:56:23.300
very tentatively an emerging pattern

00:56:23.300 --> 00:56:24.810
that in these difficult settings,

00:56:24.810 --> 00:56:26.500
it might be actually easier to change

00:56:26.500 --> 00:56:28.970
or soften some behaviors rather

00:56:28.970 --> 00:56:30.770
than self-reported attitudes,

00:56:30.770 --> 00:56:33.170
which might actually be
more difficult to shift.

00:56:35.120 --> 00:56:37.040
Or at least if we're changing attitudes,

00:56:37.040 --> 00:56:39.000
we're not doing it in a way
that we can reliably measure,

00:56:39.000 --> 00:56:40.510
like surveys are already too noisy

00:56:40.510 --> 00:56:44.923
and it's another reason why we
should move toward behaviors.

00:56:47.270 --> 00:56:49.640
So, we've touched a little
bit on generalizeability.

00:56:49.640 --> 00:56:51.653
So, just to consolidate
my thoughts on this,

00:56:51.653 --> 00:56:54.270
this was a bundled treatment in many ways.

00:56:54.270 --> 00:56:56.218
And the setting itself
was very particular.

00:56:56.218 --> 00:56:59.173
What do I think worked well?

00:56:59.173 --> 00:57:01.913
I think it worked well
that this was apolitical.

00:57:01.913 --> 00:57:04.730
I think it worked well that
this was not like a series

00:57:04.730 --> 00:57:07.490
of dialogue discussions
about the conflict itself.

00:57:07.490 --> 00:57:09.390
And it was something that
had absolutely nothing

00:57:09.390 --> 00:57:13.420
to do with politics or the conflict.

00:57:13.420 --> 00:57:15.340
That helps us with the first order problem

00:57:15.340 --> 00:57:16.980
of actually getting recruiting people

00:57:16.980 --> 00:57:18.940
to agree to an inter-group intervention.

00:57:18.940 --> 00:57:20.971
A lot of people are turned off by like,

00:57:20.971 --> 00:57:22.974
let's sit around a table and talk it out.

00:57:22.974 --> 00:57:25.480
Those interventions have their place

00:57:25.480 --> 00:57:26.740
and I think they can be powerful.

00:57:26.740 --> 00:57:28.990
But if you're wanting to optimize

00:57:28.990 --> 00:57:30.670
for a wide appeal for recruitment,

00:57:30.670 --> 00:57:32.530
that might not be the best strategy.

00:57:32.530 --> 00:57:33.920
The flip side is that, you know,

00:57:33.920 --> 00:57:35.510
I didn't see these changes to attitudes

00:57:35.510 --> 00:57:36.540
and in a way it's like, well,

00:57:36.540 --> 00:57:38.330
why should I expect them to change,

00:57:38.330 --> 00:57:40.060
to update their political beliefs?

00:57:40.060 --> 00:57:42.100
Because they didn't talk
about anything political.

00:57:42.100 --> 00:57:44.903
So, that potentially might
be a trade-off to consider.

00:57:47.200 --> 00:57:49.375
The second condition I
think we worked well here

00:57:49.375 --> 00:57:52.016
is that this was endorsed
by communal authorities.

00:57:52.016 --> 00:57:54.470
So, this is one of Allport's conditions.

00:57:54.470 --> 00:57:55.790
And so here you can see

00:57:55.790 --> 00:57:58.320
in the pictures there
that actually a priest

00:57:58.320 --> 00:58:00.293
was giving out the awards and on the right

00:58:00.293 --> 00:58:03.590
is like a rather horrifying team mascot,

00:58:03.590 --> 00:58:05.020
which I did not sign off on,

00:58:05.020 --> 00:58:06.910
but he somehow made his way into it.

00:58:06.910 --> 00:58:09.390
And he's there taking a
picture with some members

00:58:09.390 --> 00:58:11.240
of the Christian armed forces in the area.

00:58:11.240 --> 00:58:13.512
So, these guys would regularly
show up at the games.

00:58:13.512 --> 00:58:16.930
And I think this was
really important for again,

00:58:16.930 --> 00:58:19.195
in that first stage of getting
people to even sign up for

00:58:19.195 --> 00:58:22.100
them to know that this was
not gonna be stigmatizing

00:58:22.100 --> 00:58:24.210
and that this was fine
and the priests are there

00:58:24.210 --> 00:58:25.550
and the police are there

00:58:25.550 --> 00:58:28.500
and there's no taboo against doing this,

00:58:28.500 --> 00:58:30.700
given that that taboo existed beforehand,

00:58:30.700 --> 00:58:33.860
there was no contact happening
when it came to soccer.

00:58:33.860 --> 00:58:36.134
And we talked about team success.

00:58:36.134 --> 00:58:38.210
I think success is somewhat of a proxy

00:58:38.210 --> 00:58:39.350
for a positive experience,

00:58:39.350 --> 00:58:41.890
but I think it really
mattered that in general,

00:58:41.890 --> 00:58:43.280
like we made these leagues something

00:58:43.280 --> 00:58:44.400
that people really cared about

00:58:44.400 --> 00:58:46.873
and really enjoyed and listen very closely

00:58:46.873 --> 00:58:50.070
when they told us how they
wanted the leagues to look.

00:58:50.070 --> 00:58:51.840
And it helps us as researchers, right?

00:58:51.840 --> 00:58:53.970
People don't drop out. So, it's great.

00:58:53.970 --> 00:58:56.050
And so I think this

00:58:56.050 --> 00:58:58.687
all these conditions can be
generalized to other settings.

00:58:58.687 --> 00:59:03.687
And those include things like
classrooms, dorms, military,

00:59:03.770 --> 00:59:06.200
if you have conscription and workplaces,

00:59:06.200 --> 00:59:07.504
and there does seem to be

00:59:07.504 --> 00:59:11.400
this causal evidence coming
out on all of these types

00:59:11.400 --> 00:59:14.400
of settings, where they do find
positive effects of contact.

00:59:17.590 --> 00:59:20.500
So, lastly, I hope that I've showed

00:59:20.500 --> 00:59:21.860
you some causal evidence

00:59:21.860 --> 00:59:25.630
from a difficult setting
that cooperative contact

00:59:25.630 --> 00:59:28.190
can build every day social cohesion

00:59:28.190 --> 00:59:30.683
and improve everyday
interactions after war.

00:59:30.683 --> 00:59:33.840
It did seem to improve relations

00:59:33.840 --> 00:59:35.190
and behaviors between friends

00:59:35.190 --> 00:59:38.580
and acquaintances and peers
who met in the intervention.

00:59:38.580 --> 00:59:40.139
But I did find that transformative change

00:59:40.139 --> 00:59:42.070
was more difficult.

00:59:42.070 --> 00:59:45.120
So, those effects did not
necessarily extend to strangers.

00:59:45.120 --> 00:59:47.820
And so taking a step back if we want,

00:59:47.820 --> 00:59:50.470
like we can't do these contact
programs everywhere, right?

00:59:50.470 --> 00:59:52.980
So, how can we get the
biggest bang for our buck?

00:59:52.980 --> 00:59:56.020
Two suggestions here.

00:59:56.020 --> 00:59:58.120
One is to look more at spillover effects.

00:59:58.120 --> 01:00:00.450
So, this analysis I did at
the local residents suggests

01:00:00.450 --> 01:00:02.581
that there's something going
on with like being exposed

01:00:02.581 --> 01:00:05.790
or there could be something
going on with being exposed

01:00:05.790 --> 01:00:07.240
on the periphery of this contact

01:00:07.240 --> 01:00:09.205
and seeing it happen and
that being normalized

01:00:09.205 --> 01:00:11.939
and those effects extending to you.

01:00:11.939 --> 01:00:14.460
So, that's one option.

01:00:14.460 --> 01:00:16.220
And the other way to amplify the effects

01:00:16.220 --> 01:00:17.700
of contact could be pairing it

01:00:17.700 --> 01:00:21.280
with like a deeper level policy tool.

01:00:21.280 --> 01:00:23.470
So, it just might not be enough to focus

01:00:23.470 --> 01:00:24.750
only at the grassroots level,

01:00:24.750 --> 01:00:27.260
if there are these glaring
like structural inequalities

01:00:27.260 --> 01:00:29.610
that are happening outside
of the intervention.

01:00:30.840 --> 01:00:33.090
Another that I don't have
on here is also your,

01:00:33.090 --> 01:00:34.370
if you have limited resources,

01:00:34.370 --> 01:00:37.080
who should you target for
this kind of intervention?

01:00:37.080 --> 01:00:40.083
Obviously, these guys were
all, these were all guys.

01:00:41.438 --> 01:00:43.640
There were a lot of
particularities about this case

01:00:43.640 --> 01:00:45.630
that meant that it would have
been like very difficult,

01:00:45.630 --> 01:00:48.817
if not impossible to have
inter-group contact with women.

01:00:48.817 --> 01:00:53.270
But the upside of that is that it seems

01:00:53.270 --> 01:00:56.840
at least anecdotally, that
men shape the social norms

01:00:56.840 --> 01:00:57.844
in this setting.

01:00:57.844 --> 01:00:59.883
And they kind of decide what's okay

01:00:59.883 --> 01:01:01.919
and what's not okay in terms of

01:01:01.919 --> 01:01:03.920
what public spaces you can be in

01:01:03.920 --> 01:01:05.470
and who you can be friends with.

01:01:05.470 --> 01:01:07.320
And so if you want your
bang for your buck,

01:01:07.320 --> 01:01:10.220
you might want to target
the specific individuals

01:01:10.220 --> 01:01:11.881
who might have more of an
influence in that community,

01:01:11.881 --> 01:01:14.998
and then make sure you get
buy-in from the leaders as well.

01:01:14.998 --> 01:01:18.210
Actually, most of our games
happened on church fields,

01:01:18.210 --> 01:01:20.590
like the fields that were
stuck to the churches

01:01:20.590 --> 01:01:22.240
and so that was really important.

01:01:23.580 --> 01:01:26.520
And so if I have one
takeaway that I want you

01:01:26.520 --> 01:01:28.490
to leave this presentation with,

01:01:28.490 --> 01:01:31.490
it's that contact has a lot of potential,

01:01:31.490 --> 01:01:35.360
but we really need to start
measuring real-world behaviors

01:01:35.360 --> 01:01:37.500
to know what that potential
actually looks like.

01:01:37.500 --> 01:01:40.350
And we shouldn't necessarily
take it for granted

01:01:40.350 --> 01:01:43.700
that these effects will
always extend to strangers.

01:01:43.700 --> 01:01:45.870
And that might be okay in some settings,

01:01:45.870 --> 01:01:47.523
maybe in this very fragile setting,

01:01:47.523 --> 01:01:51.550
you can't expect to build
social broader social cohesion.

01:01:51.550 --> 01:01:53.930
And this localized cohesion is enough

01:01:53.930 --> 01:01:55.310
to actually build resilience

01:01:55.310 --> 01:01:57.570
against future shocks to tolerance.

01:01:57.570 --> 01:01:58.770
That's an open question.

01:02:00.110 --> 01:02:01.600
And so lastly, going back

01:02:01.600 --> 01:02:03.890
to this like classic
literature on social capital,

01:02:03.890 --> 01:02:08.120
it seems like it might be
time to revitalize the promise

01:02:08.120 --> 01:02:10.850
of these cross cutting
civic organizations,

01:02:10.850 --> 01:02:13.330
which may actually have
been so promising initially,

01:02:13.330 --> 01:02:16.210
because they included this
kind of inter-group contact,

01:02:16.210 --> 01:02:18.850
even though the original framers might not

01:02:18.850 --> 01:02:19.750
have used that language.

01:02:19.750 --> 01:02:20.900
And so there's a natural bridge

01:02:20.900 --> 01:02:22.800
to be built between those literatures.

01:02:24.140 --> 01:02:25.550
Okay. That's all I have.

01:02:25.550 --> 01:02:26.750
And I'm really looking forward

01:02:26.750 --> 01:02:28.740
to your comments and feedback,

01:02:28.740 --> 01:02:30.840
and I have a bunch of contact

01:02:30.840 --> 01:02:33.640
and other prejudice reduction
experiments in the pipeline.

01:02:33.640 --> 01:02:36.790
And so this is really the time
to have these discussions.

01:02:36.790 --> 01:02:37.657
So, thank you.

01:02:44.852 --> 01:02:45.685
- [Woman] Thank you very much Salma.

01:02:45.685 --> 01:02:46.863
Some more questions?

01:02:49.290 --> 01:02:51.500
- [Man] Yeah, I have one.

01:02:51.500 --> 01:02:54.373
So, this was super interesting,
thank you very much.

01:02:55.800 --> 01:02:58.935
So, you know,

01:02:58.935 --> 01:03:03.550
this is a local majority
community the Christians, right?

01:03:03.550 --> 01:03:05.470
Interacting with the local minority,

01:03:05.470 --> 01:03:08.380
the Muslims in a state that's historically

01:03:08.380 --> 01:03:11.500
been ruled by Muslims.

01:03:11.500 --> 01:03:14.880
And so one question is just about whether

01:03:14.880 --> 01:03:19.300
you would expect things to
work similarly elsewhere

01:03:19.300 --> 01:03:22.110
with Shiite, Sunni or
Muslims and Christians

01:03:22.110 --> 01:03:24.720
in areas where the local
majorities or minorities

01:03:24.720 --> 01:03:28.670
were different within even
the same state or whether you

01:03:28.670 --> 01:03:30.450
would expect things to
work very differently.

01:03:30.450 --> 01:03:32.010
I mean, these might be some of the things

01:03:32.010 --> 01:03:32.920
you've got in the pipeline.

01:03:32.920 --> 01:03:33.780
I don't know.

01:03:35.400 --> 01:03:37.066
- Yeah so, I think the power dynamics here

01:03:37.066 --> 01:03:39.183
were kind of quirky.

01:03:39.183 --> 01:03:43.710
I think it's another reason I
didn't have it outlined here,

01:03:43.710 --> 01:03:46.350
but one reason for this pattern
of results might also be

01:03:46.350 --> 01:03:49.026
that Christians are at the national level,

01:03:49.026 --> 01:03:53.090
in the national context are
persecuted and targeted.

01:03:53.090 --> 01:03:55.810
And so we do have some
contact work that shows

01:03:55.810 --> 01:03:58.680
that when you're looking
at people who belong

01:03:58.680 --> 01:04:01.400
to minority groups, contact
is less effective for them.

01:04:01.400 --> 01:04:03.070
They're just generally much more skeptical

01:04:03.070 --> 01:04:05.367
of the whole enterprise
and understandably so.

01:04:05.367 --> 01:04:07.910
And so that's where you really
might need the combination

01:04:07.910 --> 01:04:10.370
with policy tools to get them on board.

01:04:10.370 --> 01:04:12.000
In general, it seems to be easier

01:04:12.000 --> 01:04:15.400
for members of the majority
group to reduce their prejudice.

01:04:15.400 --> 01:04:16.233
It makes sense.

01:04:16.233 --> 01:04:18.580
It's not as consequential
to their like existence.

01:04:18.580 --> 01:04:19.710
You know, it's just kind of like, oh,

01:04:19.710 --> 01:04:21.890
I met this guy from this
group and he was cool.

01:04:21.890 --> 01:04:22.990
Okay. Move on with my life.

01:04:22.990 --> 01:04:24.239
Like, it's not so much

01:04:24.239 --> 01:04:26.430
of an inter-group threat and anxiety.

01:04:26.430 --> 01:04:28.842
So, I think it just makes
the task a bit easier.

01:04:28.842 --> 01:04:31.960
So, I would expect that
things would be easier

01:04:31.960 --> 01:04:34.740
if I focused on Muslims
as the dominant group.

01:04:34.740 --> 01:04:37.640
In terms of other types of cleavages,

01:04:37.640 --> 01:04:40.760
like ethnic, Kurdish, Arab,
for example, or within Muslims,

01:04:40.760 --> 01:04:44.860
Shiite, Sunni, that I don't
see any theoretic reason

01:04:44.860 --> 01:04:48.730
for things to be different for
those particular cleavages,

01:04:48.730 --> 01:04:51.270
but that's something I
would love to test out.

01:04:51.270 --> 01:04:54.389
And I think it's very
dependent on the on the context

01:04:54.389 --> 01:04:56.423
who seems to have the upper hand.

01:04:59.451 --> 01:05:01.330
- [Lushvig] I have another comment

01:05:01.330 --> 01:05:04.004
or a question or a suggestion.

01:05:04.004 --> 01:05:06.980
I think your work is very interesting.

01:05:06.980 --> 01:05:09.430
I would like to go back for a second

01:05:09.430 --> 01:05:13.690
