01:07:48.272 --> 01:07:52.833
Yeah, I'll follow up.

01:07:52.833 --> 01:07:54.083
- [Woman] Alex.

01:07:56.101 --> 01:07:57.880
- [Alex] Thanks very much for this site.

01:07:57.880 --> 01:07:59.310
You know, I read the paper,

01:07:59.310 --> 01:08:00.720
but the presentation really

01:08:00.720 --> 01:08:02.363
just walked me through it.

01:08:02.363 --> 01:08:03.530
I really, I learned a lot
and I'm really grateful

01:08:03.530 --> 01:08:04.720
to you for it.

01:08:04.720 --> 01:08:09.080
I wanna, I think this
presentation makes me,

01:08:09.080 --> 01:08:12.690
or the study makes me think
about Allport's conditions

01:08:14.770 --> 01:08:16.083
a little bit differently.

01:08:17.100 --> 01:08:20.629
And one is this cooperation one,

01:08:20.629 --> 01:08:22.550
you rightly pointed out, like,

01:08:22.550 --> 01:08:24.380
I don't really understand the
difference between cooperating

01:08:24.380 --> 01:08:26.020
and working towards a common goal.

01:08:26.020 --> 01:08:27.370
Okay, I agree.

01:08:27.370 --> 01:08:29.050
I can't really put my finger
on the difference between

01:08:29.050 --> 01:08:32.110
that either we could push on it,

01:08:32.110 --> 01:08:34.530
but it also you to cooperate,

01:08:34.530 --> 01:08:37.710
you need another thing, which
is the ability to cooperate.

01:08:37.710 --> 01:08:39.400
Like you can't cooperate with somebody

01:08:39.400 --> 01:08:40.830
who can't play soccer.

01:08:40.830 --> 01:08:45.096
And so like, it's a funny
subcondition of cooperation

01:08:45.096 --> 01:08:46.880
is the capacity to do that.

01:08:46.880 --> 01:08:50.380
And I think that may or
may not have been present

01:08:50.380 --> 01:08:53.230
in the previous contact
studies, like, all right,

01:08:53.230 --> 01:08:54.760
you're in a setting where
you're supposed to cooperate,

01:08:54.760 --> 01:08:56.963
but then you have the
capacity to even do so.

01:08:56.963 --> 01:08:59.680
If it's the case that you
could only make friends

01:08:59.680 --> 01:09:01.360
with people who are good at soccer,

01:09:01.360 --> 01:09:04.130
then we've got a bigger problem
than we realized, right?

01:09:04.130 --> 01:09:06.850
So, anyway, that's one of the places

01:09:06.850 --> 01:09:07.683
that we're pushing

01:09:07.683 --> 01:09:08.810
and then the other place

01:09:08.810 --> 01:09:13.810
that you brought up, is
like a weakness of the study

01:09:14.320 --> 01:09:16.409
but I don't know that it
is a weakness of the study,

01:09:16.409 --> 01:09:19.308
the extent to which the Christian players

01:09:19.308 --> 01:09:22.437
thought of the Muslim players

01:09:22.437 --> 01:09:24.540
as being typical of Muslims.

01:09:24.540 --> 01:09:27.410
And like, it's clear
that the condition here

01:09:27.410 --> 01:09:32.410
for changing attitudes is
since the attitude object

01:09:33.130 --> 01:09:37.493
is the conjured typical
Muslim in your mind.

01:09:38.410 --> 01:09:40.882
So, I wonder if in future studies,

01:09:40.882 --> 01:09:42.490
there's some measurement stuff

01:09:42.490 --> 01:09:44.200
that you could be doing there.

01:09:44.200 --> 01:09:47.999
Like, do you think that Muhammad

01:09:47.999 --> 01:09:52.560
is typical of Muslims in
your community, right?

01:09:52.560 --> 01:09:54.947
And, I would have to
imagine that they'd say, no.

01:09:54.947 --> 01:09:57.260
Like, no, Muhammad's my
friend, are you kidding?

01:09:57.260 --> 01:10:01.300
Like, so I, but then
are there interventions

01:10:01.300 --> 01:10:06.300
that you could deploy
to be like, you know,

01:10:07.950 --> 01:10:09.840
your teammates are not so different

01:10:09.840 --> 01:10:11.623
from the Muslims in your community.

01:10:12.500 --> 01:10:14.510
Like, is there a way to
explicitly draw the links

01:10:14.510 --> 01:10:17.697
to increase this typicality
inference that people are doing?

01:10:17.697 --> 01:10:19.578
It's just a suggestion for future studies.

01:10:19.578 --> 01:10:20.915
- No, this is super interesting.

01:10:20.915 --> 01:10:21.748
Like on your first point,

01:10:21.748 --> 01:10:23.421
I actually think you
might be onto something.

01:10:23.421 --> 01:10:27.110
This ability to cooperate,
yeah you actually might be.

01:10:27.110 --> 01:10:30.030
Because thinking about
like some of the studies

01:10:30.030 --> 01:10:32.645
that find like negative
or no results on contact

01:10:32.645 --> 01:10:35.430
with all the publication
biases we know about

01:10:35.430 --> 01:10:38.110
like some are mixed
classrooms in the U.S.,

01:10:38.110 --> 01:10:40.080
like these jigsaw classrooms

01:10:40.080 --> 01:10:42.620
and the other is the
recent study out of Turkey,

01:10:42.620 --> 01:10:45.490
looking at Turkish and Syrian refugee kids

01:10:45.490 --> 01:10:47.220
in like a summer camp together.

01:10:47.220 --> 01:10:48.360
And in both of those cases,

01:10:48.360 --> 01:10:49.810
in the jigsaw classes,

01:10:49.810 --> 01:10:51.590
they found that the minority students like

01:10:51.590 --> 01:10:52.930
just their ability was lower.

01:10:52.930 --> 01:10:54.968
So, or not their ability, but their,

01:10:54.968 --> 01:10:56.985
how they performed in the class

01:10:56.985 --> 01:10:59.270
was lower than their classmates.

01:10:59.270 --> 01:11:01.000
And so jigsaw puzzles means you have

01:11:01.000 --> 01:11:02.550
to all work together to solve something

01:11:02.550 --> 01:11:03.720
and then it didn't work well,

01:11:03.720 --> 01:11:06.560
maybe because it's this
underlying ability.

01:11:06.560 --> 01:11:08.114
And the second, like the study in Turkey,

01:11:08.114 --> 01:11:11.472
they like the Syrian refugee
kids don't speak much Turkish.

01:11:11.472 --> 01:11:13.807
And so it seems like ability
to cooperate in that sense.

01:11:13.807 --> 01:11:15.500
Like, you need to speak a language.

01:11:15.500 --> 01:11:20.500
Like there are some necessary
conditions for cooperation.

01:11:20.630 --> 01:11:22.800
- [Alex] Having the
Christian player switch

01:11:22.800 --> 01:11:24.530
to Arabic for crying out loud.

01:11:24.530 --> 01:11:25.450
- Exactly. Yeah.

01:11:25.450 --> 01:11:27.395
So, I actually think something to that,

01:11:27.395 --> 01:11:29.910
like the ability to cooperate
as being something different

01:11:29.910 --> 01:11:31.550
from cooperation, which I think is,

01:11:31.550 --> 01:11:33.760
I want to think about that more on.

01:11:33.760 --> 01:11:36.590
On typicality, that's
exactly what I've done

01:11:36.590 --> 01:11:37.940
in all my stuff in the pipeline now.

01:11:37.940 --> 01:11:40.110
I'm like, okay, we're
adding a typicality question

01:11:40.110 --> 01:11:45.110
in all of the end line surveys,
but more broadly, I wonder.

01:11:45.510 --> 01:11:47.590
So, I have this other study with coauthors

01:11:47.590 --> 01:11:49.780
about exposure to Hamed Salah

01:11:49.780 --> 01:11:53.610
who is an Egyptian
football, soccer superstar

01:11:53.610 --> 01:11:55.130
who plays in Liverpool.

01:11:55.130 --> 01:11:58.143
And in our survey experiment
there among fans of the club,

01:11:58.143 --> 01:12:01.190
we found that the priming
people to really think

01:12:01.190 --> 01:12:04.350
about the link between Salah
and Muslims more generally.

01:12:04.350 --> 01:12:05.904
So, when we remind them,
like he prays, he fasts,

01:12:05.904 --> 01:12:07.910
his daughter's name is Mecca.

01:12:07.910 --> 01:12:09.350
His wife wears a headscarf.

01:12:09.350 --> 01:12:12.370
Like that was the only
thing that got people

01:12:12.370 --> 01:12:14.960
to update positively about
Muslims, more broadly,

01:12:14.960 --> 01:12:17.180
as opposed to telling them he's successful

01:12:17.180 --> 01:12:20.820
or he's a really nice guy
or these other things.

01:12:20.820 --> 01:12:22.090
So, I think there's something to this,

01:12:22.090 --> 01:12:23.940
like stressing the link.

01:12:23.940 --> 01:12:25.660
Having said that I really struggle

01:12:25.660 --> 01:12:28.550
whether you view someone as typical,

01:12:28.550 --> 01:12:31.410
is that actually endogenous
to your prejudice?

01:12:31.410 --> 01:12:33.850
